If  $b,c\geq 0$  or  $b,c\leq 0$ , then one of  $\angle B$ ,  $\angle C$  is greater than  $90^{\circ}$ . WLOG  $b<0$  and  $c>0$ . We also need  $\angle BAC < 90^{\circ}$ . From lemma 1,  $\angle BAC < 90^{\circ}\implies AB^2 + AC^2>BC^2$  or  $b^2+1+c^2+1> (b-c)^2=b^2-2bc+c^2$ . So  $1>-bc$ . 

Suppose  $I$  is the incenter of  $\triangle ABC$ . It is well known that  \[I=\left(\frac{c\sqrt{b^2+1}+b\sqrt{c^2+1}}{\sqrt{b^2+1}+\sqrt{c^2+1}+c-b}, \frac{c-b}{\sqrt{b^2+1}+\sqrt{c^2+1}+c-b}\right).\]

We will try to show that the incenter is always above the lines  $y=x$  and  $y=-x$ . We claim that \[\left(\frac{c\sqrt{b^2+1}+b\sqrt{c^2+1}}{\sqrt{b^2+1}+\sqrt{c^2+1}+c-b}\right)^2<\left(\frac{c-b}{\sqrt{b^2+1}+\sqrt{c^2+1}+c-b}\right)^2.\] As the denominator is positive, we need to show  $(c\sqrt{b^2+1}+b\sqrt{c^2+1})^2< (c-b)^2$  or  $2c^2b^2+c^2+b^2+2bc\sqrt{(b^2+1)(c^2+1)}<c^2-2bc+b^2$ .

So we claim that  $b^2c^2+bc\sqrt{(b^2+1)(c^2+1)}<-bc$ . Now  $bc<0$ , so we need  $bc+\sqrt{(b^2+1)(c^2+1)}+1>0$ . We have already got that  $1>-bc$  or  $1+bc>0$ . This proves our claim. 

We now show that any point  $I=(I_x,I_y)$  above the lines  $y=x$  and  $y=-x$  will be an incenter of an acute angled triangle  $ABC$  if and only if  $I$  lies below the curve  $x^2=y^2+2y-1$ .

Construct a circle centered at  $I$  and with radius  $I_y$ . Draw tangents from  $A$  to the circle if possible. Suppose one of the line intersects  $l$  at  $x$ . 

From the tangent condition, we have  $I_x+xI_y-x=I_y\sqrt{x^2+1}\implies I_x^2+x(I_y-1)^2+2xI_x(I_y-1)=I_y^2(x^2+1)$ . This gives us a quadratic in  $x$ . If the tangents are not constructable, then the roots are complex. Suppose the roots of the equation is  $b$  and  $c$ .

Simplifying our quadratic, we have: \[x^2(1-2I_y)+x(2I_xI_y-2I_x)+I_x^2-I_y^2=0.\] 

We want an acute angled triangle, so  $1>-bc$ . From vieta's,  $bc=\frac{I_x^2-I_y^2}{1-2I_y}$ . So we need \[1>\frac{I_y^2-I_x^2}{1-2I_y}\implies 1-2I_y>I_y^2-I_x^2\implies I_x^2>I_y^2+2I_y-1.\]

So the quadratic gives required roots only when  $I$  lies below the curve and when  $I$  lies below the curve, we can solve the quadratic to get two solutions which shows that such a  $I$  is constructible.

Note that the boundaries is not part locus because the inequalities are strict.</details>

