We then construct a graph  $G''$  by taking any pair of double edges, 	deleting one of them, and adding any missing edge of  $G''$  in its place. 	(This is always possible, since when  $m = \binom k2$  we have  $n-1 \ge k$  	and when  $m = \binom k2 +1$  we have  $n-1 \ge k+1$ .)

Thus we have arrived at a simple graph  $G''$  	with one fewer vertex. 	We also observe that we have  $S(G'') \ge S(G)$ ; 	after all every vertex in  $G''$  has degree 	at least as large as it did in  $G$ , 	and the  $d$  edges we deleted have been replaced with 	new edges which will have labels at least  $d$ . 	Hence we may apply the inductive hypothesis to the graph  $G''$  	to obtain  $G^\ast$  with  $S(G^\ast) \ge S(G'') \ge S(G)$ .  $\blacksquare$ The problem then is completed once we prove the following:**Claim**:  	For any graph  $G$ , 	[list]
- If  $G$  has  $\binom k2$  edges for  $k \ge 3$ , 		then  $S(G) \le \binom k2 \cdot (k-1)$ .
- If  $G$  has  $\binom k2 + 1$  edges for  $k \ge 4$ , 		then  $S(G) \le \binom k2 \cdot (k-1) + 1$ . 	[/list]



[i]Proof.*  We prove both parts at once by induction on  $k$ , with the base case  $k = 3$  being plain (there is nothing to prove in the second part for  $k=3$ ). Thus assume  $k \ge 4$ . By the earlier lemma, we may assume  $G$  has a universal vertex  $v$ . For notational convenience, we say  $G$  has  $\binom k2 + \varepsilon$  edges for  $\varepsilon \in \{0,1\}$ , and  $G$  has  $p+1$  vertices, where  $p \ge k-1 + \varepsilon$ .

Let  $H$  be the subgraph obtained when  $v$  is deleted. Then  $m = \binom k2 + \varepsilon - p$  is the number of edges in  $H$ ; from  $p \ge k-1+\varepsilon$  we have  $m \le \binom{k-1}{2}$  and so we may apply the inductive hypothesis to  $H$  to deduce  $S(H) \le \binom{k-1}{2} \cdot (k-2)$ .



[asy] defaultpen(fontsize(11pt)); size(4.5cm);

