Since  $\overline{CR}$ ,  $\overline{CO}$  isogonal, we have that  $\angle RCA=90^\circ - \angle A$ , so  $\overline{CR}$  is an altitude. Thus  $\triangle DRC$  is right with circumcenter  $S$ . Then both  $L$  and  $S$  lie on the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{RC}$ , which proves that  $\overline{LS}\parallel \overline{AB}$ . 

Now let  $T$  be the reflection of  $L$  across  $S$ ; by symmetry across  $\overline{OM}$ ,  $T$  lies on  $(ABC)$  and line  $\overline{BPR}$ . We will show  $T$  is the desired point of concurrency. Project   $$ -1=(A, B; M, \infty)\stackrel{S}{=} (R, B; P, T), $$  so  $P$  lies on the polar of  $T$  with respect to both  $(ABD)$  and  $(BIC)$ . Then  $\overline{TL}\perp \overline{XPY}$  implies that  $\overline{XY}$  is the polar of  $T$ , so  $\overline{TX}$  and  $\overline{TY}$  are tangent to  $(BIC)$ .

To finish, Power of a Point at  $P$  gives   $$ PX\cdot PY=PR\cdot PB=PA\cdot PD, $$  so  $AXDY$  cyclic. Then observe that   $$ TY=TX=LX=LB=TA, $$  so  $T$  is the circumcenter of  $AXDY$ . But  $T$  also lies on the radical axis of  $(BAD)$  and  $(BIC)$ ! Hence the power of  $T$  with respect to  $(BAD)$  is  $TA^2=TD^2$ , which proves that  $TA$ ,  $TD$  are tangent to  $(BAD)$ , as desired.

Firstly, note that if  $N$  is the midpoint of  $AC$ , then  $\angle AQM=\angle ANM=B$ , so  $AMNQ$  is cylic. But  $(AMN)$  has diameter  $AO$ , so  $Q$  is the foot from  $A$  to  $CO$ . Also, if  $L$  is the arc midpoint of  $BC$  on  $(ABC)$ , then  $(BIC)$  has center  $L$ . These two observations allow us to draw an accurate diagram, as well as get rid of  $I$ .
