Needed a few hints to solve this. I think I still solved most of it on my own though.

Let  $T$  be the midpoint of arc  $BC$  of  $(ABC),$  and let  $P$  be the reflection of  $T$  over  $MO.$  Note that  $$ AP=BT=CT, $$  so both  $ABTP$  and  $APCT$  are isosceles trapezoids, so we have that  $AC=PT.$  Note that there is a circle centered at  $P$  that passes through  $X,$   $Y,$  and  $A.$  Let  $N$  be the midpoint of  $AC.$ Then, note that  $$ \measuredangle AQM=\measuredangle ACB=\measuredangle ANM, $$  so  $AMQN$  is cyclic. Additionally, by a homotlety of ratio  $\frac{1}{2}$  at  $A,$  we have that  $O$  lies on  $(AMQN)$  such that  $AO$  is a diameter. Thus, we have that  $$ \measuredangle AQC=\measuredangle AQO=90^\circ. $$  Then, note that by the radical axis theorem on  $(AQC),$   $(BIC),$  and  $(XYA),$  we have that  $AY,$   $CQ,$  and the radical axis of  $(XYA)$  and  $(AQC)$  are concurrent at  $AY\cap CQ= O.$  Thus, the radical axis of  $(XYA)$  and  $(AQC)$  passes through  $O,$  so there exists a point  $F\neq A$  such that  $F$  lies on  $(AQC),$   $(XYA),$  and  $AO.$ Note that  $$ NA=NF\text{ and }PA=PF\implies NP\perp AF=AO, $$  so quadrilateral  $ANOP$  is an ortiodiagonal quadrilateral. We then have that
\begin{align*}
AP^2+ON^2&=AN^2+OP^2 
AP^2+OA^2-AN^2&=AN^2+OP^2 
AP^2&=2AN^2 
AP&=AN\sqrt{2} 
AC&=AP\sqrt{2} 
PT&=PY\sqrt{2}.
\end{align*}
Note that  $QX=QY=PX=PY,$  so because  $PT=PY\sqrt{2}$  we have that  $PXTY$  is a square. This means that  $PX$  and  $PY$  are tangent to  $(BIC).$ Then, note that by the Radical Axis Theorem on  $(ABC),$   $(BIC),$  and  $(PXTY),$  we have that there exists a point  $G=PT\cap MO\cap BC,$  and note that by the definition of  $P$  we have that  $G$  is the midoint of  $PT.$  We also have  $DT\parallel CP$  so  $CPDT$  is a parallelogram, so  $$ PA=CT=PD. $$  We can also find that  $$ \measuredangle PAD=\measuredangle PAT=\measuredangle PBT=\measuredangle ABC-\measuredangle ABP-\measuredangle CBT=\measuredangle ABC=\measuredangle ABD, $$  so  $PA$  is tangent to  $(ABD).$  Because  $PA=PD,$  we have that  $PD$  is also tangent to  $(ABD).$  Thus, we have that the four lines described in the problem statement concur at  $P,$  which we have clearly lies on  $(ABC).$  This finishes the problem.

Another construction for b):  $n=k(k+1)^2(k+2)^2(k+3)$  satisfies the conditions since
\begin{align*}
(x+k(k+2)(k+3))(x+(k+1)^2(k+2)) &= x^2+(2k^3+9k^2+11k+2)x+n 
(x+(k+1)^2(k+3))(x+k(k+2)^2) &= x^2+(2k^3+9k^2+11k+3)x+n 
(x+(k+1)(k+2)^2)(x+k(k+1)(k+3)) &= x^2+(2k^3+9k^2+11k+4)x+n 
\end{align*}

Yet another construction for part  $(b):$ If  $n=x(x+1)^2(x+2)(2x+1)(2x+3),$  we claim that  $\mathcal{F}(n)$  has three consecutive elements. Notice that for any  $t|a,$  we have  $t+\tfrac{n}{t} \in \mathcal{F}.$  So the elements  $$ (x+1)^2(2x+1)+x(x+2)(2x+3)=4x^3+12x^2+10x+1 $$    $$ x(x+1)(2x+3)+(x+1)(x+2)(2x+1)=4x^3+12x^2+10x+2 $$    $$ x(x+2)(2x+1)+(x+1)^2(2x+3)=4x^3+12x^2+10x+3 $$  are all consecutive terms in  $\mathcal{F}(n),$  so we done.  $\square$ **P.S.** This was pretty easy to come up with. <details><summary>Here's</summary>Let the three consecutive elements of  $\mathcal{F}(n)$  be  $a+\tfrac{n}{a}, b+\tfrac{n}{b}, c+\tfrac{n}{c}.$  So  $$ b+\frac{n}{b}=a+\frac{n}{a}+1 \Leftrightarrow \left( 1+\frac{1}{a-b} \right)ab=n $$  So write  $y=a-b.$  Similarly  $x=b-c$  so that  $a=c+x+y.$  Then
\begin{align*}
\left(1+\frac{1}{y} \right)ab=n=\left(1+\frac{1}{x} \right)bc &\implies \frac{x+1}{x}c=\frac{y+1}{y}a 
&\Leftrightarrow  \frac{c+x+y}{c}=\frac{a}{c}=\frac{(x+1)y}{x(y+1)} 
&\Leftrightarrow \frac{x+y}{c}=\frac{y-x}{x(y+1)} 
&\Leftrightarrow c=\frac{x(y+1)(x+y)}{y-x}
\end{align*}
Since we want  $c \in \mathbb{N},$  we set  $y-x=1.$  So using  $b=c+x, a=c+x+y,$  we get  $$ a=y(x+1)(x+y), b=xy(x+y+2), c=x(y+1)(x+y) $$  where  $y=x+1.$  So we can easily get  $$ n=\frac{x+1}{x} bc=\frac{x+1}{x} \cdot xy(x+y+2) \cdot x(y+1)(x+y)=xy(x+1)(y+1)(x+y)(x+y+2) $$  Now put in  $y=x+1$  and we done!</details> how.

**<span style="color:#f00">Claim: </span>**  $\mathcal F(n) = \{ d + \frac{n}{d} : d \mid n \}$ *Proof:* Note  $m \in \mathcal F(n) \iff m^2 - 4n$  is a perfect square. Write  $m^2 - 4n = q^2$  with  $q \in \mathbb Z_{\ge 0}$ . Then,   $$ (m+q)(m-q) = 4n $$  Note  $m = \frac{(m+q) + (m-q)}{2}$ . This forces  $m+q,m-q$  to have same parity, and since there product is even, so they are both odd. Let  $m+q = 2d$ , so  $m-q = \frac{2n}{d}$ . Then  $m = d + \frac{n}{d}$ . Clerly  $m \in \mathbb Z \iff d \mid n$ .   $\square$ Now assume that   $$   \left\vert \left( d_1 + \frac{n}{d_1} \right) - \left( d_2 + \frac{n}{d_2} \right) \right\vert = 1  $$  WLOG  $d_1d_2 \le n$ , otherwise change  $d_2 \to \frac{n}{d_2}$ . WLOG,  $d_1 < d_2$ . Now, 
\begin{align*}
1 &= \left( d_1  + \frac{n}{d_1} \right) - \left( d_2 + \frac{n}{d_2} \right) = (d_2 - d_1) \left( \frac{n}{d_1d_2} - 1 \right)
\end{align*}
Let  $d_1 - d_2 = k$  and  $d_1 = y$ . Then  $d_2 = y+k$  and   $$  n = \frac{d_1d_2(k+1)}{k} = \frac{y(y+k)(k+1)}{k}  $$  Note  $y,y+k \mid n \iff k \mid y$ . Write  $y = kx$ . That would force  $$ n = x(x+1) \cdot k(k+1) $$  Conversely, if  $n = x(x+1) \cdot k(k+1)$  then divisors  $kx,kx+k$  yield two consecutive numbers of  $\mathcal F(n)$ . We conclude that   $$  S = \{x(x+1) \cdot k(k+1) : x,k \in \mathbb Z_{>0} \}  $$  This implies their infinitude (say, by fixing  $x$  and varying  $k$ ). Now,   $$  \sum_{n \in S} \frac{1}{n} \textcolor{blue}{\le} \left( \sum_{x \ge 1} \frac{1}{x(x+1)} \right) \left( \sum_{k \ge 1} \frac{1}{k(k+1)} \right)= 1 \cdot 1 = 1 $$  Note that  $\textcolor{blue}{\le}$  cannot be (at least directly) be strengthened to  $=$  because of double counting. This solves (a), now we solve (b). 

We put  $d_3 = d_2 + (k+1) = kx + k + (k+1)$  so that we have   $$  \left(d_2 + \frac{n}{d_2} \right) - \left( d_3 + \frac{n}{d_3} \right)  = (d_3 - d_2) \left( \frac{n}{d_2d_3} - 1 \right)  $$  This would yield   $$  n = \left( \frac{xk + k}{k+1} \right) \left( \frac{xk + k}{k+1} + 1 \right) \cdot (k+1)(k+2)   $$  We will show for  $x = (k+2)(2k+1)$ , this value of  $n$  and the previous value  $x(x+1) \cdot k(k+1)$  are equal. Indeed, 
\begin{align*}
& x(x+1) \cdot k(k+1) = \left( \frac{xk + k}{k+1} \right) \left( \frac{xk + k}{k+1} + 1 \right) \cdot (k+1)(k+2) 
&\iff x(k+1) = \left( \frac{xk + 2k + 1}{1} \right) (k+2) 
&\iff x(k+1)^2 = k(k+2)x + (2k+1)(k+2) 
&\iff x = (k+2)(2k+1)
\end{align*}
This gives infinite solutions for (b), completing the proof.  $\blacksquare$

**<span style="color:#f00">Part (a)</span>** By Vieta's we can write two polynomials,
\begin{align*}
P_1(x) &= (x - r) \left(x - \frac{n}{r} \right)
P_2(x) &= (x - s) \left(x - \frac{n}{s} \right)
\end{align*}
Note then that we have,
\begin{align*}
\left(r + \frac{n}{r} \right) + 1 &= \left(s + \frac{n}{s} \right)
n\left(\frac{s - r}{rs} \right) &= s - r - 1
n &= \frac{rs(1 + r - s)}{(r - s)}
\end{align*}
Set  $r - s = k$  so we have,
\begin{align*}
n &= \frac{s(s + k)(k + 1)}{k}
\end{align*}
Note then  $k \mid s$  so let  $s = mk$  to find,
\begin{align*}
n &= mk(m + 1)(k + 1)
\end{align*}
Thus we have,
\begin{align*}
\sum_{n \in S} \frac{1}{n} \leq \left( \sum \frac{x}{x+1} \right)^2 = 1
\end{align*}
thus proven.  $\square$ **<span style="color:#00f">Part (b)</span>** Say the three pairs of roots are,  $\left( \frac{n}{a}, a \right)$ ,  $\left(\frac{n}{b}, b \right)$ ,  $\left( \frac{n}{c}, c\right)$ . Then we require,
\begin{align*}
a + \frac{n}{a} + 1 &= b + \frac{n}{b}
b + \frac{n}{b} + 1 &= c + \frac{n}{c}
\end{align*}
Dealing with just the first equation we find,
\begin{align*}
n\left( \frac{1}{a} - \frac{1}{b} \right) &= b - a - 1
n\left( \frac{b - a}{ab} \right) &= b - a - 1
n &= \frac{ab(a - b + 1)}{a - b}
\end{align*}
However we can similarly find,
\begin{align*}
n &= \frac{bc(b - c + 1)}{b - c}
\end{align*}
Now write  $a = b + x$  and  $b = c + y$ . Then we have,
\begin{align*}
n &= \frac{b(b + x)(x + 1)}{x} = \frac{c(c + y)(y + 1)}{y}
\end{align*}
However from the last two equations we have,
\begin{align*}
\frac{(c + y)(c + x + y)(x + 1)}{x}& = \frac{c(c + y)(y + 1)}{y}
\frac{c + x + y}{c} &= \frac{(y + 1)x}{(x + 1)y}
\frac{x + y}{c} &= \frac{x - y}{(x+1)y}
c &= \frac{(x + 1)(x+y)y}{x - y}
\end{align*}
So setting  $x = y + 1$  we have,
\begin{align*}
c &= y(y + 2)(2y + 1)
\end{align*}
works. This gives  $b = y(y + 2)(2y + 1) + y$ , from which,
\begin{align*}
n =\boxed{ y (y + 1) (y + 2) (2 y + 1) (2 y^2 + 5 y + 3) }
\end{align*}
and we're done.

We claim the answer is  $\frac{4^n - 1}{3}$ .

We first prove the bound. First notice that the hop sizes are in  $\{2^0, 2^1, \ldots, 2^{n - 1}\}$ , since the frog must stay within bounds the whole time. Let  $a_i$  be the number of hops of size  $2^i$  the frog makes, for  $0\le i\le n - 1$ .**Claim**:  	For any  $k = 1, \dots, n$  we have 	\[ a_{n-1} + \dots + a_{n-k} \le 2^n - 2^{n-k}. \]

*Proof.*  	Let  $m = n-k$  and look modulo  $2^m$ . 	Call a jump *small* if its length is at most  $2^{m-1}$ , 	and *large* if it is at least  $2^m$ ; 	the former changes the residue class of the frog modulo  $2^m$  	while the latter does not.

Within each fixed residue modulo  $2^m$ , 	the frog can make at most  $\frac{2^n}{2^m} - 1$  large jumps. 	So the total number of large jumps is at most 	 $2^m \left( \frac{2^n}{2^m} - 1 \right) = 2^n - 2^m$ .  $\blacksquare$ (As an example, when  $n = 3$  this means there are at most four hops of length  $4$ , at most six hops of length  $2$  or  $4$ , and at most seven hops total. Of course, if we want to max the length of the hops, we see that we want  $a_2 = 4$ ,  $a_1 = 2$ ,  $a_0 = 1$ , and in general equality is achieved when  $a_m = 2^m$  for any  $m$ .)

Now, the total distance the frog travels is \[ S = a_0 + 2a_1 + 4a_2 + \dots + 2^{n-1} a_{n-1}. \] We rewrite using the so-called ``summation by parts'': \begin{align*}
	S &= a_0 + a_1 + a_2 + a_3 + \dots + a_{n-1} 
	&+ a_1 + a_2 + a_3 + \dots + a_{n-1} 
	&+ 2a_2 + 2a_3 + \dots + 2a_{n-1} 
	&+ 4a_3 + \dots + 4a_{n-1} 
	&+\vdots 
	&+ 2^{n-2}a_{n-1}. 
\end{align*}
Hence
\begin{align*}
	S &\le (2^n-2^0) + (2^n-2^1) + 2(2^n-2^2) + \dots + 2^{n-2}(2^n-2^{n-1}) 
	&= \frac{4^n-1}{3}.
\end{align*}


It remains to show that equality can hold. There are many such constructions but most are inductive. Here is one approach. We will construct two family of paths such that there are  $2^k$  hops of size  $2^k$ , for every  $0\le k\le n - 1$ , and we visit each of  $\{0, \ldots, 2^n - 1\}$  once, starting on  $0$  and ending on  $x$ , for the two values  $x\in\{1, 2^n - 1\}$ .

The base case  $n = 1$  is clear. To take a path from  $0$  to  $2^{n+1}-1$ .  	
- Take a path on  $\{0, 2, 4, \ldots, 2^{n+1} - 2\}$  	starting from  $0$  and ending on  $2$  (by inductive hypothesis).
- Take a path on  $\{1, 3, 5, \ldots, 2^{n+1} - 1\}$  	starting from  $1$  and ending on  $2^{n+1}-1$  (by inductive hypothesis).
- Link them together by adding a single jump  $2 \to 1$ .
 The other case is similar, but we route  $0 \to (2^{n+1}-2) \to (2^{n+1}-1) \to 1$  instead. (This can also be visualized as hopping along a hypercube of binary strings; each inductive step takes two copies of the hypercube and links them together by a single edge.)**Remark** (Ashwin Sah):  The problem can also be altered to ask for the minimum value of the sum of the reciprocals of the hop sizes, where further we stipulate that the frog must hit every point precisely once (to avoid triviality). With a nearly identical proof that also exploits the added condition  $a_0 + \cdots + a_{n - 1} = 2^n - 1$ , the answer is  $n$ . This yields a nicer form for the generalization. The natural generalization changes the above problem by replacing  $2^k$  with  $a_k$  where  $a_k|a_{k + 1}$ , so that the interval covered by hops is of size  $a_n$  and the hop sizes are restricted to the  $a_i$ , where  $a_0 = 1$ . In this case, similar bounding yields \[\sum_{i = 1}^{2^n - 1}\frac{1}{b_k}\ge\sum_{i = 0}^{n - 1}\left(\frac{a_{k + 1}}{a_k} - 1\right).\] Bounds for the total distance traveled happen in the same way as the solution above, and equality for both can be constructed in an analogous fashion.

Why is this problem so hard...

Clearly jump sizes are  $\in \{2^0, \ldots 2^{n -1}\}$  because the frog is bound by  $[1, 2^n - 1]$ . Suppose the frog makes a  $2^i$  move  $a_i$  times, for each  $i$  from  $0$  to  $n-1$ . We wish to minimize\[\sum_{i = 0}^{n-1} 2^ia_i.\]In fact, I claim that we can place the following bound:**<span style="color:#f00">Claim:</span>** We have  $a_{n - k} + \ldots + a_{n-1} \leq 2^{n} - 2^{n-k}$  for all  $k$  from  $1$  to  $n$ . **<span style="color:#f00">Proof:</span>** We want to show that the number of jumps whose size is  $\geq 2^{n.- k}$  is at most  $2^{n-k}(2^k - 1)$ . Suppose we split the number line section from  $0$  to  $2^n - 1$  into  $2^k$  sections of modulo  $2^{n - k}$  residue classes, for example\[\{0, \ldots , 2^{n-k} - 1\} \cup \{2^{n-k}, \ldots , 2^{n - k + 1} - 1\} \cup \ldots \cup \{2^{n} - 2^{n - k}, \ldots , 2^n - 1\}.\]For each residue modulo  $2^{n - k}$  the frog can jump to at most  $2^k - 1$  different sections (not including the original) and there are  $2^{n - k}$  residues, hence a total of  $\leq 2^{n-k}(2^k - 1) = 2^n - 2^{n - k}$  such jumps as desired.  $\square$ Now, we wish to maximize
\begin{align*} 
\sum_{i = 0}^{n-1} 2^ia_i &= a_0 + 2a_1 + 4a_2 + \ldots + 2^{n-1}a_{n-1}&\leq S_0 + S_1 + 2S_2 + 4S_3 + \ldots + 2^{n-2}S_{n-1}
\end{align*}
where  $S_i = a_i + \ldots + a_{n-1}$ . Now substitute in  $S_i \leq 2^n - 2^{i}$  for all  $i$  from  $0 \to n - 1$ . We evaluate as follows:\begin{align*}S_0 + 2S_2 + 4S_3 + \ldots + 2^{n-2}S_{n-1} &= (2^n - 2^0) + (2^n - 2^1) + \ldots + 2^{n-2} \cdot (2^n - 2^{n-1})
&= (1 + 1 + 2 + \ldots + 2^{n-2}) \cdot 2^n - (2^0 + 2^1 + 2^3 + 2^5 + \ldots + 2^{2n - 3})&= 2^{2n-1} - \left(1 + 2\cdot \frac{4^{n-1} - 1}{3}\right)&= \frac{4^n - 1}{3}\end{align*}so indeed our maximum has been established. I am too lazy to post the construction.

The answer is  $\frac{4^n-1}{3}$ . We first show that it is always attainable.

Proceed by induction on the statement: " $\frac{4^n-1}{3}$  is attainable no matter which of  $0,1,2,\ldots, 2^n-1$  you start on." The base case  $n=1$  is trivial, either  $1$  to  $0$  or  $0$  to  $1$ , giving  $\frac{4^1-1}{3}$ . For the inductive step, if we start at any number  $i\in\{0,1,\ldots, 2^{k+1}-1\}$ , take the sequence of moves that worked for  $n=k$  starting at  $\left\lfloor \frac{i}{2}\right\rfloor$ , except double the length of every hop. Then, move by  $2^0$  to the left or right to  $j$ , and we repeat. Thus, starting at any  $i$ , we have travelled  $$ \frac{4^k-1}{3}\times 2\times 2+1=\frac{4^{k+1}-4}{3}+1=\frac{4^{k+1}-1}{3} $$  completing the inductive step.

To prove the bound, let  $a_{n-1}, a_{n-2},\ldots, a_0$  be the number of jumps of  $2^{n-1},2^{n-2},\ldots, 2^0$  respectively. By counting the number of pairs  $(i,i+2^j)$  for each  $j$  that the frog has not been to:
\begin{align*}
a_{n-1}&\leq 2^{n-1}
a_{n-1}+a_{n-2}&\leq 2^{n-1}+2^{n-2}
&\vdots
a_{n-1}+a_{n-2}+\ldots +a_0 &\leq 2^{n-1}+2^{n-2}+\ldots+ 2^{0}
\end{align*}
The distance that the frog travels is:  $$ d=2^{n-1}a_{n-1}+2^{n-2}a_{n-2}+\ldots+2^0a_0\leq 2^{n-1}\cdot 2^{n-1}+2^{n-2}\cdot 2^{n-2}+\ldots +2^0\cdot 2^0=\frac{4^n-1}{4-1} $$  by the rearrangement inequality, as desired.

The answer is  $\tfrac{4^n-1}{3}$ . **Construction:** We show that  $\tfrac{4^n-1}{3}$  is always achievable by induction on  $n$ . The base case of  $n = 1$  is trivial. Assume that there exists a path of length  $\tfrac{4^{k}-1}{3}$  for  $n = k$ , and let this path be denoted as  $\{0, a_1, a_2, \dots, a_{\ell}, 1\}$ . Now, we construct the path \begin{align*} \{0 \to 2a_1 \to 2a_2 \to \cdots \to 2a_{\ell} \to 2\} \cup \{3 \to 2a_{\ell} + 1 \to \cdots, 2a_{1}+1 \to 1\} \end{align*} 
which has length  $2 \cdot \tfrac{4^k-1}{3} + 2 \cdot \tfrac{4^k-1}{3} + 1 = \tfrac{4^{k+1}-1}{3}$ .  $\square$ <span style="color:#f00">**Claim:**</span> A move of length  $\ge 2^k$  can be used at most  $2^n - 2^k$  times. 
*Proof.* Moves of length  $\ge 2^k$  preserve the number  $\pmod{2^k}$ , so only moves that have length less than  $2^k$  can change its residue  $\pmod{2^k}$ . There are  $2^k$  residues that must be achieved, which means there must be at least  $2^k$  moves of length less than  $2^k$ , which directly implies the result.  $\square$ To finish, let  $s_k$  denote the number of moves of length  $2^k$ . The above claim can be modeled as \begin{align*} s_k + s_{k+1} + \cdots + s_n \le 2^n - 2^k ~~~ (\clubsuit)\end{align*}

Now, we multiply this equation by  $2^{k-1}$  for  $k > 0$ , while leaving the  $k = 0$  equation as it is. Now, summing up all versions of  $(\clubsuit)$  gives \begin{align*} 2^{0}s_0 + 2^{1}s_1 + \cdots + 2^{n}s_n \le \tfrac{4^n-1}{3}\end{align*}
as desired.  $\blacksquare$

The answer is  $1+4+4^2+\cdots+4^{n-1} = \tfrac{4^n-1}3$  hops.**<span style="color:blue">Construction:**</span> We induct on  $n$ ; the  $n=1$  case is clear. Suppose that  $a_1, a_2, \dots, a_{2^n-1}$  are the numbers the frog reaches to make  $\tfrac{4^n-1}3$  jumps. Then, the sequence \[S = \left(2^n, 2^n+a_1, a_1, a_2, 2^n+a_2, \dots, 2^n + a_{2^n-1}, a_{2^n-1}\right)\] yields a total jump length of  $\tfrac{4^n-1}3 + 4^n = \tfrac{4^{n+1}-1}3$ , as needed.**<span style="color:blue">Bound:**</span> The key claim is the following:**<span style="color:#f00">Claim:**</span> For each  $0 \leq k \leq n-1$ , the frog makes at most  $2^n-2^k$  jumps of length at least  $2^k$ .

*Proof:* Suppose otherwise, that the frog makes  $a > 2^n-2^k$  jumps of length at least  $2^k$ . Then the frog makes  $2^k-\ell < 2^k - 1$  jumps of length at most  $2^{k-1}$ , i.e. there are  $\ell - 1$  residues  $r$  modulo  $2^k$  such that the frog never reaches numbers of residue  $r$ . In particular, there are  $(\ell-1) 2^{n-k}$  numbers the frog never reaches. Thus \[2^n - \ell + 1 \leq a + 2^k - \ell \leq 2^n - (\ell-1)2^{n-k}.\] On the other hand \[2^n - (\ell-1)2^{n-k} \leq 2^n - 2(\ell-1) \leq 2^n - \ell\] which is a contradiction.  $\blacksquare$ For each  $0 \leq k \leq n-1$ , suppose that the frog makes  $a_k$  jumps of length  $n-1-k$ . The claim implies that  $S_k = a_0+a_1+\cdots+a_k \leq 2^n - 2^{n-1-k}$  for each  $k$ . Thus 
\begin{align*}
A &= 2^{n-1}a_0 + 2^{n-2}a_1+ \cdots + a_{n-1} 
&= S_{n-1} + S_{n-2} + 2S_{n-3} + \cdots + 2^{n-2} S_0 
&\leq 2^{2n-1} + 2^{2n-1} - \frac{2\left(4^n-1\right)}3 - 1 
&= \frac{4^n-1}3
\end{align*}
as needed.

I wrote this solution kind of like a program :)

The answer is all but  $2^m - 1$ . **Proof of all but  $2^m - 1$  work:**

int x = 1; 

Perform the following operation infinitely
{
Add  $x$  to the set of values that work. 
Consider the unique prime  $p \mid b^{2x} - 1$  and  $p \nmid b^{x} - 1$  (which exists by Zsigmondy's). 

Note that  $p^2 \mid b^{2px} + 1$  since  $\nu_p(b^{2px} - 1) \ge 2$  by LTE and  $\nu_p(b^{px} - 1) = 0$  as that would imply  $b^x \equiv 1 \pmod p$ . Let  $x = xp$ . }

We can keep doing this so to produce infinite values that work. **Proof all others don't:** Note that for the smallest prime that divides  $b^n + 1$ , the order must divide  $\gcd(2n, p - 1) = \gcd(2, p - 1)$  (since p is smallest prime) or 2. If the order is 1, then  $1 \equiv -1 \pmod p$ , so p must be 2. If the order is 2,  $p \mid b^2 - 1$  and  $p \nmid b - 1$  so  $p \mid b + 1$  and is  $p$  is 2 again. However, this means  $n$  is even and that implies  $b^n \equiv 1 \pmod 4$  and  $b^n + 1 \equiv 2 \pmod 4$  which isn't a multiple of  $n^2$  ever, contradiction.

We claim that  $\boxed{\text{all } b \text{ not of the form } 2^k -1 \text{ are good.}}$ We first try to determine  $b$  for which there may exist infinitely such good  $n$ . Say we have a satisfactory base case of  $n$  for some  $b$ . Then we wish to find some prime  $p \nmid n$  such that  $p^2 \mid b^{np} + 1$ . **<span style="color:#f00">Claim:</span>** We can find a satisfactory infinite sequence of  $n$  for  $b \neq 2$  and  $b \neq 2^k - 1$  for some  $k$ , using Zsigmondy.

*Proof.* Consider taking  $p \neq 2$  as a primitive divisor of  $b^{2n} - 1$ . Then note that  $p \nmid n$  follows as  $n \mid b^{\phi(n)} - 1$  and  $p \nmid b^{\phi(n)}-1$ . Also clearly  $p \mid b^n + 1$  as  $p \nmid b^n - 1$ . Then we find,
\begin{align*}
b^{np} \equiv b^n \equiv - 1\pmod{p}
\end{align*}
Note that LTE gives  $\nu_p(b^{np} + 1) = \nu_p(b^n+1) + \nu_p(p) \geq 2$ . Hence  $p^2 \mid b^{np}+1$ . 

Now note this argument fails for  $n$  in which we are unable to find a base case, or where our base case violates Zsigmondy's. Namely when we have,


- The only solution for some  $b$  such that  $b + 1$  is a power of  $2$  is  $n = 2$ .
- The only solution when  $b = 2$  is  $n = 3$

Thus the claim holds.  $\blacksquare$ Now we in fact show that for  $b = 2$  or  $ b =2^k - 1$  there exists no satisfactory  $n$ . **<span style="color:#00f">Claim:</span>** For  $ b =2^k - 1$  or  $b = 2$  there exists no satisfactory  $n$  for a base case.

*Proof.* We will handle the first bullet to begin with. Assume that  $b = 2^k - 1$  for some  $k$ . Then we wish to find  $n \neq 2$  such that  $n^2 \mid (2^k - 1)^n + 1$ . Taking the smallest prime  $p$  dividing  $n$ , which we will assume is greater than  $2$ . Then we must have,
\begin{align*}
(2^k - 1)^n &\equiv - 1 \pmod{p}
(2^k - 1)^{2n} &\equiv 1 \pmod{p}
\end{align*}
Then  $\text{ord}_p(2^k - 1) \mid \gcd(2n, p - 1) = 2$  from which we conclude either  $\text{ord}_p(2^k - 1) = 1$  of  $\text{ord}_p(2^k - 1) = 2$ . Testing the first case we find we then have,
\begin{align*}
2^k - 1 &\equiv 1 \pmod{p}
\iff 2^k &\equiv 2 \pmod{p}
\iff 2^{k-1} &\equiv 0 \pmod{p} \iff p = 2
\end{align*}
Testing the second case we have,
\begin{align*}
2^{2k} - 2^{k + 1} &\equiv 0 \pmod{p}
2^{2k} &\equiv 2^{k+1} \pmod{p}
2^{k - 1} &\equiv 0 \pmod{p} \iff p = 2
\end{align*}
Thus we must have  $2 \mid n$ . However then note that then  $4 \mid (2^k - 1)^{n} + 1$ , but we have  $(2^k - 1)^n + 1 \equiv 2 \pmod{4}$ , a contradiction. Thus indeed whenever  $b + 1$  is a power of  $2$  there are no satisfactory values of  $n$ . 

To handle the second bullet, note that it is just [90IMO3](https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c6h60740p366466), and we see that  $n = 3$  is indeed the only solution, so  $b = 2$  fails. Hence our claim holds.  $\blacksquare$ We will now present a base-case construction for all other  $b$ . 

For some  $b$  consider taking  $n$  as the smallest odd prime divisor  $p$  of  $b + 1$ . Then LTE we find,
\begin{align*}
\nu_p(b^p + 1) = \nu_p(b + 1) + \nu_p(p) \geq 2
\end{align*}
Thus we're done.**Remark:** I felt like this problem was just stress testing Zsigmondy, after using the 00IMO5 argument. Also big thank you to **nebula** on the OTIS discord server for hinting me along on this problem.

Neat mix of a bunch of olympiad NT techniques.


I claim that the answer is all positive integers  $b$  of the form  $2^c-1$  where  $c$  ranges across all positive integers greater than or equal to  $2.$  

Firstly, suppose that  $b=2^c-1$  for some  $c \ge 2.$  If  $n$  is even, then we will have   $$ b^n+1 \equiv 2 \pmod{4}, $$   but also   $$ 4 \mid b^n+1, $$   contradiction. Therefore,  $n$  is odd. 

Then, let  $p$  be the smallest prime dividing  $n.$  We have
\begin{align*}
    b^n \equiv -1 \pmod{p} &\implies b^{2n} \equiv 1 \pmod{p} 
    &\implies \text{ord}_p(b) \mid \gcd(2n, p-1).
\end{align*}
Since  $p$  is the smallest prime dividing  $n,$  we know that  $p-1$  and  $2n$  share no prime factors other than  $2,$  so it results that  $\text{ord}_p(b)$  is  $1$  or  $2.$  If it is  $2,$  then we have   $$ b \equiv -1 \pmod{p} \implies p=2^c, $$   contradiction. Thus,  $\text{ord}_p(b)$  must actually be  $1,$  implying that   $$ b \equiv 1 \pmod{p} \implies b^n \equiv 1 \pmod{p}, $$   contradiction. 

Hence, if  $b$  is one less than a power of  $2,$  than there exists no positive integer  $n>1$  such that  $n^2 \mid b^n+1,$  implying that there are finitely many such positive integers. It remains to show that if  $b$  is not one less than a perfect power, then there exist infinitely many positive integers  $n$  such that  $n^2 \mid b^n+1.$  

Note that if we let  $p$  be a prime factor of  $b+1,$  then we will have   $$ p^2 \mid b^p+1. $$   Now, it suffices to show that if there exists some positive integer  $n$  such that   $$ n^2 \mid b^n+1, $$   then there exists some prime  $q$  not dividing  $n$  such that   $$ (nq)^2 \mid b^{nq}+1. $$   By Zsigmondy's Theorem, since  $b$  is not one less than a power of  $2,$  there exists some prime  $q$  such that  $q \mid b^{2n}-1$  and  $q \nmid b^k-1$  for all  $1 \le k <2n.$  This implies that   $$ b^n \equiv -1 \pmod{q}. $$   Now, if  $q \mid n,$  then suppose that  $n=qj.$  By Fermat's Little Theorem, we have
\begin{align*}
    b^{qj} \equiv -1 \pmod{q} &\implies b^j \equiv -1 \pmod{q} 
    &\implies b^{2j} \equiv 1 \pmod{q} 
    &\implies q \mid b^{2j}-1.
\end{align*}
However, we have  $2j<2n,$  contradicting  $2n$  being the smallest possible value of  $y$  satisfying  $q \mid b^y-1.$  Since   $$ n^2 \mid b^{n}+1, q \mid b^n+1, $$   and  $q \nmid n,$  it results that   $$ n^2q \mid b^n+1, $$   so the Lifting the Exponent Lemma yields   $$ n^2q^2 \mid b^{nq}+1. $$   

Hence, I have shown that if there is some positive integer  $n$  such that   $$ n^2 \mid b^n+1, $$   then if  $b$  is not one less than a power of  $2,$  there exists some prime  $q$  not dividing  $n$  such that   $$ (nq)^2 \mid b^{nq}+1. $$   This produces an infinite number of desired positive integers, as claimed, concluding the proof.  $\square$

A few people have asked me how I created the problem, so I will offer some comments. Some accounts may be omitted, exaggerated, or wrong.

I was originally trying to write an AMC/AIME-level problem involving an equilateral triangle in some way. Eventually, I decided on the following.
<blockquote>
Let  $ABC$  be an equilateral triangle with side 2 inscribed in circle  $\omega$ , and let  $P$  be a point on small arc  $AB$  of its circumcircle. The tangent line to  $\omega$  at  $P$  intersects lines  $AC$  and  $AB$  at  $E$  and  $F$ . If  $PE = PF$ , find  $EF$ .
[asy]
pair A = dir(90), B = dir(210), C = dir(330);
pair P = point(unitcircle, intersections(unitcircle, (A+B)/2, (A+C)/2)[1]);
pair E = extension(P, P * (1 + I), A, C);
pair F = extension(P, P * (1 + I), A, B);
draw(unitcircle);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(A--E^^B--F, gray(0.7));
draw(E--F);

add(pathticks(P--E, 1));
add(pathticks(P--F, 1));

dot(A^^B^^C^^P^^E^^F);
label(" $A$ ", A, dir(60));
label(" $B$ ", B, dir(270));
label(" $C$ ", C, dir(330));
label(" $P$ ", P, dir(P));
label(" $E$ ", E, dir(P));
label(" $F$ ", F, dir(P));
[/asy]
</blockquote>
This problem is not difficult. However, I was intrigued because the answer is 4, which means  $PE = PF$  is the side of the triangle. (Exercise: prove this as simply as possible!)

In the interim, I tried to find more properties of this configuration. After some time playing around in GeoGebra, I discovered that the reflection of  $A$  over  $P$  lies on line  $BC$ .[asy]
pair A = dir(90), B = dir(210), C = dir(330);
pair P = point(unitcircle, intersections(unitcircle, (A+B)/2, (A+C)/2)[1]);
pair E = extension(P, P * (1 + I), A, C);
pair F = extension(P, P * (1 + I), A, B);
pair Ap = 2*P - A;
draw(unitcircle);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(A--E^^B--F, gray(0.7));
draw(E--F);
draw(A--Ap, dotted);
draw(B--Ap, dotted);

add(pathticks(P--E, 1));
add(pathticks(P--F, 1));
add(pathticks(P--A, 2));
add(pathticks(P--Ap, 2));

dot(A^^B^^C^^P^^E^^F^^Ap);
label(" $A$ ", A, dir(60));
label(" $B$ ", B, dir(270));
label(" $C$ ", C, dir(330));
label(" $P$ ", P, dir(P));
label(" $E$ ", E, dir(P));
label(" $F$ ", F, dir(P));
[/asy]
Of course, this is equivalent to  $P$  being on the  $A$ -midline. At this point, it was natural to conjecture that we can relax the condition on  $\triangle ABC$  to only  $AB = AC$ , and it turns out this property still holds. So we get the following problem.
<blockquote>
Let  $ABC$  be an isosceles triangle with  $AB = AC$ , and let  $M$  be the midpoint of  $\overline{BC}$ . Let  $P$  be a point on the circumcircle with  $PA = PM$ . The tangent to the circumcircle at  $P$  intersects lines  $AC$  and  $AB$  at  $E$  and  $F$ , respectively. Show that  $PE = PF$ .
</blockquote>
After some time, I was able to find the following solution (hidden for length).

<details><summary>Solution</summary>[asy]
unitsize(100);
pen n_purple = rgb(0.7,0.4,1),
n_blue = rgb(0,0.6,1),
n_green = rgb(0,0.4,0),
n_orange = rgb(1,0.4,0.1),
n_red = rgb(1,0.2,0.4);
pair A, B, C, M, P, D, E, F;
A = dir(90);
B = dir(190); C = reflect(A, origin) * B;
M = (B + C)/2;
P = (-sqrt(1 - (1 + M.y)^2/4), (1 + M.y)/2);
D = extension(P, A, B, C);
E = extension(A, C, P, rotate(90, P) * origin);
F = extension(A, B, P, rotate(90, P) * origin);
draw(B--F^^A--E^^D--B, gray(0.5));
draw(P--B^^P--C, gray(0.7));
draw(P--A^^P--M^^P--D);
draw(E--F);
draw(unitcircle);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);
dot(A^^B^^C^^M^^P^^D^^E^^F);

label(" $A$ ", A, dir(60));
label(" $B$ ", B, dir(130));
label(" $C$ ", C, dir(350));
label(" $M$ ", M, dir(270));
label(" $P$ ", P, P);
label(" $D$ ", D, dir(270));
label(" $E$ ", E, P);
label(" $F$ ", F, P);
[/asy]
First of all, we contend  $PA = PM = \sqrt{PB \cdot PC}$ . Let the external bisector  $PA$  of  $\angle BPC$  intersect line  $BC$  at  $D$ ; then by a well-known result ( $\triangle PBD \sim \triangle PAC$ , or  $\sqrt{bc}$  inversion),  $PA \cdot PD = PB \cdot PC$ . However,  $PA = PM$  implies  $PM = PD$ , so  $PA = PD$  and  $PA^2 = PB \cdot PC$ , as desired.

Next, by a well-known result,  $\tfrac{EA}{EC} = (\tfrac{PA}{PC})^2$  and  $\tfrac{FB}{FA} = (\tfrac{PB}{PA})^2$ . Since  $PA = \sqrt{PB \cdot PC}$ , in fact  $\tfrac{EA}{EC} = \tfrac{FB}{FA}$ . As  $AB = AC$ , this implies  $EA = FB$  and  $EC = FA$ . Then
\[PE^2 = EA \cdot EC = FB \cdot FA = PF^2\]
as desired.</details>

I was happy with this solution; I felt like I was doing nontrivial geometry, so I showed the problem to Evan. While he believed it was nice, he was worried that people would destroy the problem by coordinates. So I suggested the following variant.
<blockquote>
Let  $ABC$  be an isosceles triangle with  $AB = AC$  and circumcircle  $\omega$ , and let  $M$  be the midpoint of  $\overline{BC}$ . Let  $P$  be a point on  $\omega$  with  $PA = PM$ . The tangent to  $\omega$  at  $P$  intersects lines  $AC$  and  $AB$  at  $E$  and  $F$ , respectively. Show that the circumcircle of  $\triangle AEF$  passes through the center of  $\omega$ .
</blockquote>
To solve this after  $PE = PF$ , note that it follows that the center  $O$  of  $\omega$  is equidistant from  $E$  and  $F$ , and  $\overline{AO}$  externally bisects  $\angle EAF$ , so  $O$  is the arc midpoint. Both of us were happy about this change, and we moved on to bigger and better things in life.

Well, that's not really true. Over a month later, I returned to the problem above (v2), and noticed it was true for *any* triangle  $ABC$ . Oops.
<blockquote>
