Now we prove the other part. Set  $O' = \odot(AE_1F_1) \cap \odot(AE_2F_2)$ . By radical axes stuff, it suffices to show  $O'$  is the center of  $\omega$ . Note that a spiral similarity  $\tau$  at  $O'$  maps  $\triangle O'F_1E_1 \mapsto O'F_2E_2$ . But since  $F_1E_2 = F_2E_2$ , so  $\tau$  must be a rotation. Now since  $\tau$  maps  $F_1 \mapsto F_2$  maps  $E_1 \mapsto E_2$ , so we must have  $O'F_1 = O'F_2$  and  $O'E_1 = O'E_2$ . In particular,  $O'$  is the intersection of perpendicular bisector of segments  $F_1F_2 , E_1E_2$ . But since  $F_1B = F_2A$  and  $E_1A = E_2C$ , so the perpendicular bisector of segment  $F_1F_2$  is also the perperndicular bisector of segment  $AB$  and the perpendicular bisector of segment  $E_1E_2$  is also the perpendicular bisector of segment  $AC$ . This gives that  $O'$  is the intersection of perpendicular bisector of segments  $AB,AC$ , implying  $O'$  is the center of  $\omega$ .

