So this means that the fact that  $PQ$  cuts segments  $AB,AC$  at their midpoints has something to do with the problem and we need to exploit it somehow. I first though of constructing the center of the circle since it would give us a lot of right and 4 cyclic quadrilaterals. So I tried some radical axes stuff with these 4 cyclic quad and I didn't get anything (unfortunately, I didn't even think of angle chasing which would directly solve the problem, as in first solution of Evan Chen  :( ). I also tried computing lengths of segments  $PN,QM$  using power of a point but their lengths came to be something in square roots, which is not nice.

Then I though exploiting that is not in the reach of normal stuff so I have to try some projective geo. So I though of some perspectivities and I saw that dropping those tangents would give me parallel lines. So I did that construction since tangents are also good. Then I found that symmetry thing which immediately gave me the first result. 


Then I had to solve the second part and it seemed that it has nothing to do with our tangent constructions. First I made a conjecture that we only need  $E_1F_1 = E_2F_2$ . But that easily failed.

Then was natural to define the second intersection of circles since we know simply nothing about their centers and we also got some spiral similarities. Finally, I got that nice stuff about perpendicular bisectors thing and the problem was solved!</details>

