Per usual, we use homothety to show that  $Z$  is the midpoint of  $PD.$  (Alternatively, just use SAS similarity on  $\triangle DXY$  and  $\triangle DEF,$  and then look at the  $P$  and  $Z$  as convenient points collinear with  $D.$ ) Because of this midpoint, we notice that  $AZ$  is the perpendicular bisector of  $PD,$  so  $A$  is the circumcenter of  $\triangle EPD.$  
Now, for the bash, we put  $A,B,C,D$  on the unit circle, and to ensure that they are perpendicular, we set:  $$ \begin{cases}A=e^{ia}
B=e^{ib}
C=e^{-ia}
D=-e^{-ib}=e^{i(\pi-b)}\end{cases} $$  Because  $AD=AQ,$  we know that  $\overarc{AD}=\overarc{AQ}$  on the circumcircle of  $ABCD,$  so the arguments of  $D,A,Q$  should form an arithmetic progression. This lets us define  $Q$ :  $$ Q=e^{i(2a+b-\pi)}=-e^{i(2a+b)}. $$  We define  $E$  the typical way with reflections (I'm boring).
\begin{align*}
\left(\dfrac{E-B}{A-B}\right)&=\overline{\left(\dfrac{D-B}{A-B}\right)}
E&=B+\dfrac{\frac1D-\frac1B}{\frac1A-\frac1B}\cdot(A-B)
&=B+\dfrac{\frac1D-\frac1B}{\frac{B-A}{AB}}\cdot(A-B)
&=B+A-\frac{AB}D
&=e^{ib}+e^{ia}-e^{i(a+2b-\pi)}
&=e^{ib}+e^{ia}+e^{i(a+2b)}.
\end{align*}
Before finding  $E-Q,$  we take a detour because we would like to show  $|E-Q|=|D-P|$  (so that we may ignore the rest of the diagram). Recall that because  $P$  is the reflection of  $D$  over  $Z,$   $$ |P-D|=2|\cos(a)-(-\cos(b))|=2|\cos(a)+\cos(b)| $$  because  $PD$  is parallel to the real axis. Now we evaluate  $|E-Q|.$ \begin{align*}
|E-Q|&=\left|e^{ib}+e^{ia}+e^{i(a+2b)}-\left(-e^{i(2a+b)}\right)\right|
&=\left|e^{i(a+b)}\right|\left|e^{-ia}+e^{-ib}+e^{ib}+e^{ia}\right|
&=2\left|\cos(a)+\cos(b)\right|.
\end{align*}
Similar holds for the other side of the diagram, so we are done.</details>

