<details><summary>Solution</summary>Let  $N = AC \cap BD,$  and let  $X$  and  $Y$  be the feet of the altitudes from  $D$  to  $AB$  and  $AC,$  respectively. Since  $BD \perp AC,$  we have that  $N$  is the foot of the altitude from  $D$  to  $AC.$  By Simson Lines, we know that  $X, N, Y$  are collinear. Moreover, by construction, we also have that  $D,X,E$  are collinear, as are  $D,Y,F.$  On top of that, we know that  $DE = 2DX$  and  $DF = 2DY.$  Therefore, there exists a homothety  $h$  of scale  $2$  centered at  $D$  mapping  $X$  to  $E$  and  $Y$  to  $F.$ In particular, since  $X,N,Y$  are collinear and  $E,P,F$  are as well by the definition of  $P,$  we must also have that  $h(N) = P,$  so  $DN = NP.$  Therefore, by SAS congruence,  $\triangle ANP \cong \triangle AND,$  so  $AP = AD.$  However, since  $AD = AE,$  we have that  $A$  is, in fact, the circumcenter of  $\triangle EDP.$  As a consequence,  $Q \ne D$  is the unique point on  $\omega$  such that  $AD = AQ.$  Moreover, by the Reflecting the Orthocenter lemma, if  $H$  is the orthocenter of  $\triangle ABC,$  then  $AH = AD.$  This means that  $P$  is the orthocenter of  $\triangle ABC.$ On top of that, since  $AQ = AP,$  and  $Q$  lies on  $\omega,$  this lemma also tells us that  $Q$  is the reflection of  $P$  across  $AB,$  so  $PQ \perp AB.$  Since  $PC \perp AB$  due to  $P$  being the orthocenter, we obtain the miraculous fact that  $CQ \perp AB.$  Similarly,  $AR \perp BC.$ There have been a few solutions that length-bashed from here, but when mocking this JMO, I finished using a complex bash. Since  $BD \perp AC,$  it is a standard fact that   $$ ac + bd = 0, $$   so  $d = -\frac{ac}{b}.$ We will now find  $e$  and  $f$  in terms of  $a,b,c.$  From what we know about complex numbers, the foot of the altitude form  $D$  to  $AB$  is   $$ x = \frac{1}{2} (a+b+d-ab\overline{d}). $$   Since  $D,X,E$  are collinear with  $DX = XE,$  we have that 
\begin{align*}
e = 2x - d = a + b + d - ab\overline{d} - d &= a + b - ab\overline{d} 
&= a + b - \frac{ab}{d} 
&= a + b - \frac{ab}{-\frac{ac}{b}} 
&= a + b + \frac{b^2}{c},
\end{align*} where the step  $\overline{d} = \frac{1}{d}$  comes from the fact that  $d$  lies on the unit circle. Similarly,   $$ f = b + c + \frac{b^2}{a}. $$   Now we will find  $p$  and  $q.$  Since  $CQ \perp AB,$  we have   $$ cq + ab = 0, $$   so  $q = \frac{-ab}{c}.$  Similarly,  $r = \frac{-bc}{a}.$ We then have that   $$ e - q = a + b + \frac{b^2}{c} - \left(-\frac{ab}{c}\right) = \frac{b^2 + ab + bc + ca}{c} $$   and   $$ f - r = b + c + \frac{b^2}{a} - \left(-\frac{bc}{a}\right) = \frac{b^2 + ab + bc + ca}{a}. $$   Finally, we have   $$ EQ = |e - q| = \frac{|b^2 + ab + bc + ca|}{|c|} = \frac{|b^2 + ab + bc + ca|}{|a|} = |f-r| = FR, $$   since  $|c| = |a| = 1,$  so we are done.</details>

