*Proof.* Angle-chasing, we get
\begin{align*}
C,P,Q \text{ collinear} &\iff \angle{PQD}+\angle{CQD}=180^\circ 
&\iff 180^\circ - \angle{PED} + \angle{CAD} = 180^\circ 
&\iff \angle{PED} = \angle{CAD} 
&\iff \angle{MHD} = \angle{MAD}.
\end{align*}
Now, note that  $\angle{AMD}=\angle{AHD}=90^\circ,$  so  $AHMD$  is cyclic. This implies that  $\angle{MHD}=\angle{MAD},$  concluding the proof of this lemma.  $\blacksquare$  

Analogously, we know that  $A, P,$  and  $R$  are collinear. Next, we prove the following lemma:**<span style="color:red">Claim:</span>**  $A$  is the circumcenter of  $(EDP).$ *Proof.* Since  $M$  and  $H$  are the midpoints of  $\overline{DP}$  and  $\overline{DE},$  respectively, and   $$ \angle{AMD}=\angle{AHD}=90^\circ, $$   we know that  $A$  lies on the perpendicular bisectors of  $\overline{DP}$  and  $\overline{DE}.$  Therefore, it results that  $A$  is the circumcenter of the circumcircle of  $EDP,$  as desired.  $\blacksquare$ Analogously,  $C$  is the circumcenter of  $(FPD).$  We now prove our final lemma:**<span style="color:red">Claim:</span>**  $QPED$  is an isosceles trapezoid. 
*Proof.* It suffices to prove that  $QP \parallel DE.$  Angle-chasing using the first two lemmas gives us
\begin{align*}
QP \parallel DE &\iff \angle{QPD} = \angle{PDE} 
&\iff \angle{CPD}=\angle{PDE} 
&\iff \angle{CDP} = \angle{PDE} 
&\iff \angle{CDB} = \angle{BDH} 
&\iff 90^\circ - \angle{ACD} = \angle{BDA} - \angle{ADH} 
&\iff 90^\circ - \angle{ACD} = \angle{BDA} - 90^\circ+\angle{BAD} 
&\iff \angle{BAD} + \angle{ABD}+\angle{BDA} = 180^\circ.
\end{align*}
Hence, since all cyclic trapezoids are isosceles, we have proven the claimed result.  $\blacksquare$ Analogously,  $PRDF$  is an isosceles trapezoid. The diagonals of isosceles trapezoids have equal lengths, so we get   $$ EQ=PD=FR, $$   as desired.  $\square$

