I agree with the fact that this is a weird choice; the ideas of mediants and Farey fractions are decently well-known.  (In fact, the official solution cites the mediant result as a remark.  My guess is that the people constructing the exams didn't realize that idea was already well-known.)  For what it's worth though, the official solution isn't bad at all, and I think it might be good to replicate it here.

<details><summary>Official Solution</summary>The first inequality is equivalent to  $9p > 5q$ , and because both sides are integers, it follows that  $9p - 5q\geq 1$ .  Similarly,  $4q - 7p\geq 1$ .  Now \begin{align*}\frac{1}{63}
 = \frac47 - \frac59& = \left(\frac pq - \frac59\right) + \left(\frac 47 - \frac pq\right) &= \frac{9p-5q}{9q} + \frac{4q - 7p}{7q}&\geq\frac{1}{9q} + \frac{1}{7q} = \frac{16}{63q}.\end{align*} Thus  $q\geq 16$ .  Because \[\frac{8}{16} < \frac 59 < \frac{9}{16}
 < \frac 47 < \frac{10}{16},\] the fraction  $\tfrac{9}{16}$  lies in the required interval, but  $\tfrac{8}{16}$  and  $\tfrac{10}{16}$  do not.  Therefore, when  $q$  is as small as possible,  $q=16$  and  $p=9$ , and the requested difference is  $16 - 9 = 7$ .</details>

