<details><summary>Solution</summary>We will use orders and modular arithmetic.

\begin{align*}
2019^8+1 &\equiv 0\pmod{p}
2019^8 &\equiv -1\pmod{p}
2019^{16} &\equiv 1\pmod{p}
\end{align*}

Now FLT implies that  $\mathrm{ord}_p {2019}$  must divide  $16$ , but it also cannot divide  $8$  by the same logic, so it must be  $16$ . Thus,  $16\vert p-1$ .

Now we test primes, because some of them might not satisfy the condition for the reason that their order might be a proper factor of  $16$ . First, we test  $17$ .

\[2019^8\equiv319^8\equiv(-4)^8\equiv16^4\equiv(-1)^4\equiv1\pmod{17}\]

This is not what we wanted, so we try the next prime, which happens to be  $97$ :

\[2019^8\equiv79^8\equiv(-18)^8\equiv324^4\equiv33^4\equiv1089^2\equiv119^2\equiv22^2\equiv484\equiv-1\pmod{97}\]

This works, so the answer is  $\boxed{97}$ .</details>

