Denote by  $a$  the side length of the regular octagon. The circular segment has area  $\frac 18 - \frac{\sqrt 2}{4\pi}$  by a trivial computation, and notice that 
\begin{align*}
\text{dist}(P, \overline{A_1A_2}) &= \frac 1a\left(\frac 1{28} + \frac{\sqrt 2}{2\pi}\right) 
\text{dist}(P, \overline{A_3A_4}) &= \frac 1a\left(-\frac 1{36} + \frac{\sqrt 2}{2\pi}\right).
\end{align*}
The essence of the problem is computing  $\text{dist}(P, \overline{A_2A_3})$ . Using Pythagoras directly is computationally infeasible (I tried multiple times, but failed), so we employ a more creative approach. Toss the problem onto the coordinate plane, with
\begin{align*}
A_1 &= \left(-\frac 12 a, \frac 12 ka\right) 
A_2 &= \left(\frac 12 a, \frac 12ka \right) 
A_3 &= \left(\frac 12 ka, \frac 12 a\right) 
A_4 &= \left(\frac 12 ka, -\frac 12 a\right).
\end{align*}
Here  $k = \tan \frac{3\pi}8$ . Then notice that   $$ P =\left (\frac 12 ka - \frac 1a\left(-\frac 1{36} + \frac{\sqrt 2}{2\pi}\right), \frac 12 ka - \frac 1a\left(\frac 1{28} + \frac{\sqrt 2}{2\pi}\right)\right), $$   so by the point to line distance formula, \begin{align*} \text{dist}(P, \overline{A_2A_3}) &= -\left(\frac{ka - \frac 1a\left(\frac 1{126} + \frac{\sqrt 2}{\pi}\right )- \frac 12(a+ka)}{\sqrt 2} \right)
&= -\left(\frac{\sqrt 2}4 ka - \frac{\sqrt 2}4 a - \frac{\sqrt 2}{252 a} - \frac 1{a\pi}\right).
\end{align*}
We take the negative sign, as  $P$  obviously lies on the lesser side of the line. Note  $\frac{\sqrt 2}4 \left(\tan \frac{3\pi} 8 - 1 \right) = \frac 12$ , and 
\begin{align*}
\text{dist}(P, \overline{A_6A_7}) &= (\sqrt 2+1) a - \left(-\frac 12 a+\frac{\sqrt 2}{252a} + \frac 1{a\pi}\right) 
&= \sqrt 2 a +  \frac 32 a - \frac{\sqrt 2}{252 a} - \frac 1{a\pi}.
\end{align*}
So the area 
\begin{align*}
[PA_6A_7] &= \frac 12 a\left(\sqrt 2 a +  \frac 32 a - \frac{\sqrt 2}{252 a} - \frac 1{a\pi}\right) 
&= \frac{2-\sqrt 2}{\pi}\left(\frac{\sqrt 2}2 + \frac 34\right) - \frac{\sqrt 2}{504} - \frac 1{2\pi} 
&= \frac{\sqrt 2}{4\pi} + \frac 1{2\pi} - \frac 1{2\pi} - \frac{\sqrt 2}{504}  
&= \frac{\sqrt 2}{4 \pi} - \frac {\sqrt 2}{504}.
\end{align*}
Adding back the sector, we finally arrive at our answer of  $\frac 18 - \frac{\sqrt 2}{\boxed{504}}$ , and we are done.

