Using casework on the flips:**Case 1:**  $x$  is 0 or 1.
This occurs half of the time, and then no matter what if flipped for  $y$  on the first coinflip, there is a half chance that  $|x-y|>\frac12$ . So Case 1 occurs with a probability of  $\frac12\cdot\frac12=\frac14$ .**Case 2:**  $x$  is in the interval.
If  $y$  is either 0 or 1, then there is a half chance that  $|x-y|>\frac12$ . This occurs with a probability of  $\left(\frac12\right)^2\cdot \frac12=\frac18$ . Otherwise, we have the following diagram:
[asy]
filldraw((0,0)--(0,1)--(1/2,1)--(0,1/2)--cycle,green+linewidth(1));
filldraw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1/2)--(1/2,0)--cycle,green+linewidth(1));
draw((0,0)--(0,1)--(1,1)--(1,0)--cycle);
[/asy]
The area of the shaded region is  $\frac14$ . Therefore, the probability that this case occurs is  $\left(\frac12\right)^2\cdot\frac14=\frac1{16}$ Adding them all together, we have  $$ \frac14+\frac18+\frac1{16}=\boxed{\text{(B) }\frac{7}{16}} $$

