Let  $y=\sqrt{1-(x-2)^2}$  represent the rightmost semicircle. The big circle is then given by  $y=1\pm\sqrt{4-x^2}$ , and it intersects the x-axis at  $(\pm\sqrt{3},0)$ . The area of the intersection of the semicircle, circle, and x-axis can then be found by subtracting  $\int_{\sqrt{3}}^2 \left(1-\sqrt{4-x^2}\right)\mathrm{d}x$  from the area of a quarter circle. <details><summary>Evaluating this integral</summary>\begin{align*}
\int \left(1-\sqrt{4-x^2}\right)\mathrm{d}x &= \int 1\mathrm{d}x - \int\sqrt{4-x^2}\mathrm{d}x 
&= x-\int\sqrt{4-x^2}\mathrm{d}x
\end{align*}
Let  $x=2\sin{\left(u\right)}\implies\mathrm{d}x=2\cos{\left(u\right)}\mathrm{d}u$ \begin{align*}
&= x-\int 2\cos{\left(u\right)}\sqrt{4-4\sin^2{\left(u\right)}}\mathrm{d}u
&= x-\int 2\cos{\left(u\right)}\sqrt{4\cos^2{\left(u\right)}}\mathrm{d}u
&= x-4\int \cos^2{\left(u\right)}\mathrm{d}u
&= x-4\int \frac{1+\cos{\left(2u\right)}}{2}\mathrm{d}u
&= x-4\left(\frac{1}{2}u+\frac{\sin{\left(2u\right)}}{4}\right)
&= x-4\left(\frac{1}{2}u+\frac{\cos{\left(u\right)}\sin{\left(u\right)}}{2}\right)
&= x-2u-2\cos{\left(u\right)}\sin{\left(u\right)}
&= x-2\arcsin{\left(\frac{x}{2}\right)}-2\cos{\left(\arcsin{\left(\frac{x}{2}\right)}\right)}\sin{\left(\arcsin{\left(\frac{x}{2}\right)}\right)}
&= x-2\arcsin{\left(\frac{x}{2}\right)}-x\sqrt{1-\frac{x^2}{4}}
\end{align*}</details> gives  $\left[x-2\arcsin{\left(\frac{x}{2}\right)}-x\sqrt{1-\frac{x^2}{4}}\right]_{\sqrt{3}}^2=2-\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}-\frac{\pi}{3}$ . By symmetry, the desired area is thus  $4\pi-\left(\frac{\pi}{2}+2\left(\frac{\pi}{4}-\left(2-\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}-\frac{\pi}{3}\right)\right)\right)=\frac{7}{3}\pi+4-\sqrt{3}\implies\boxed{\textbf{(E) }17}$ .

