<u>Claim 1:</u> The points  $BCXY$  and  $UVXY$  are cyclic.

<u>Proof:</u> Let  $\psi$  be inversion at  $P$  sending  $\Gamma$  to itself. Let  $K=\psi(M)$ . Note that  $X'\equiv\psi(X)=KC\cap BP$  and  $Y'\equiv\psi(Y)=KB\cap CP$ . Similarly  $U'\equiv\psi(U)=KC\cap\Gamma$  and  $V'\equiv\psi(V)=KB\cap\Gamma$ .

Note that the Cevians  $BX'$  and  $CY'$  of  $\triangle KBC$  are concurrent on the the median  $KM$  of that triangle, so  $X'Y'\parallel BC$  (one can see this by applying Ceva's theorem). Since  $BCDE$  is cyclic, we have then by Reim's theorem that  $X'Y'DE$  is cyclic. Similarly, since  $BCU'V'$  cyclic, we have by Reim's theorem that  $X'Y'U'V'$  is cyclic. Inverting back, we see that  $XYBC$  and  $XYUV$  are cyclic, as desired.  $\blacksquare$ <u>Claim 2:</u> As  $P$  varies, the points  $U$  and  $V$  are fixed.

<u>Proof:</u> This is a classic application of Reim's theorem. Let  $C'$  be the point on  $\Gamma$  such that  $CC'\parallel AM$ , and redefine  $U$  as  $C'M\cap\Gamma$ , which is independent of  $P$ . Note that  $DUCC'$  cyclic and  $CC'\parallel PM$ , so  $DUPM$  cyclic by Reim's theorem, as desired. We can similarly show that  $V$  is independent of  $P$ .  $\blacksquare$ <u>Claim 3:</u> For any point  $S$  on  $\Gamma$ , the circumcircle of  $(SXY)$  passes through a fixed point as  $P$  varies.

<u>Proof:</u> This portion is just radical axis. Let  $T\equiv BC\cap UV$ . By Claim 1, we see that  $TB\cdot TC=TU\cdot TU\cdot TV=TX\cdot TY$ . Thus,  $T$  is the radical center of  $\Gamma$ ,  $(BCXY)$ , and  $(SXY)$ , so  $T$  is on the radical axis of  $(SXY)$  and  $\Gamma$ . Letting  $H=TS\cap\Gamma$ , we see then that  $H\in(SXY)$ , which is our desired fix point.  $\blacksquare$ Taking  $S=L$  solves the problem.

