<span style="color:#0000ff">**Claim:**</span> Line  $XY$  passes through a fixed point on  $BC$ .

Proof: By Menelaus on  $\Delta BCW$  it suffices to show that  $\tfrac{WX}{XC} \cdot \tfrac{BY}{YW}$  is fixed. Note that   $$ WX = \frac{WP \cdot WM}{WC} = \frac{(2LW-LP)LM}{LB} $$   and   $$ XC = WC-WX=BL-\frac{(2LW-LP)LM}{LB}. $$  Using similar substitutions for  $BY$  and  $YW$ , we get   $$ \frac{WX}{XC} \cdot \frac{BY}{YW} = \frac{\frac{LM(2LM-LP)}{BL}}{BL-\frac{LM(2LM-LP)}{BL}} \cdot \frac{LC-\frac{LM(2LM-LP)}{LC}}{\frac{LM(2LM-LP)}{LC}} = \frac{LC^2+x-2LM^2}{LB^2+x-2LM^2} $$  where  $x=LM \cdot LP$  is the power of  $P$  wrt  $\Gamma$ . Substituting  $x=LO^2-(MO^2+MC^2)$  where  $O$  is the center of  $\Gamma$ , we get from the law of cosines that   $$ \frac{LC^2+x-2LM^2}{LB^2+x-2LM^2} = \frac{(LM^2+MC^2-LC^2)+(LM^2+MO^2-LO^2)}{(LM^2+MB^2-LB^2)+(LM^2+MO^2-BO^2)} = \frac{MO \cos(\angle LMO)+MC\cos( \angle LMC)}{MO \cos(\angle LMO) + MO \cos( \angle LMB)} $$   which is independant of the location of  $L$  as desired.
