    &P(2,q):2^{f(q)} + q^2=f(q)^2+2^q  \Rightarrow  2^{f(q)}- f(q)^2=2^q-q^2 
\end{align*}
The function  $2^x-x^2$  is increasing function in interval  $[3, \infty]$  since  $f'(x)= \ln (x) 2^x-2x \ge 0 \ \forall x \in [3,\infty]$   Therefore if  $f(q) \neq q$  we have   $ 2^{f(q)}- f(q)^2>2^q-q^2$  or  $ 2^{f(q)}- f(q)^2<2^q-q^2  $ . Hence either  $f(q)=2$  or  $f(q)=q$ . Obviously,  $f(q) \neq 2$  hence  $f(q)=q$   $\blacksquare$

