If  $\triangle{ABC}$  is equilateral, then it is obvious that  $A', B',$  and  $C'$  lie at the midpoints of the sides of  $\triangle{ABC},$  clearly implying that  $\triangle{A'B'C}$  is equilateral. 

Now, it remains to show that if  $\triangle{A'B'C}$  is equilateral, then  $\triangle{ABC}$  is also equilateral. Firstly, since the median to the hypotenuse in any right triangle has length equal to half the length of the hypotenuse, we know that   $$ B'C=B'A'=B'C'=B'A. $$   This implies that  $B'$  is the center of a circle containing  $A, A', C,$  and  $C'.$  Therefore, we have
\begin{align*}
    \angle{BCA'}=\angle{ACC'} &\implies \angle{A'CC'}=\angle{ACC'} 
    &\implies \angle{C'A'A}=\angle{C'AA'} 
    &\implies C'A'=C'A.
\end{align*}
Hence, we have  $AB'=B'C'=C'A',$  so  $\triangle{AB'C'}$  is equilateral. This implies that   $$ \angle{A}=60^\circ. $$   

Then, we also have  $\angle{AB'C'}=\angle{C'B'A'}=60^\circ,$  so it follows that  $\angle{A'B'C}=60^\circ.$  Since  $B'A'=B'C,$  we then get   $$ \angle{B}=\angle{A'CB'}=60^\circ. $$   Hence, we have   $$ \angle{A}=\angle{B}=\angle{C}=60^\circ, $$   from which ot follows that  $\triangle{ABC}$  is equilateral, concluding the proof.  $\square$

