Simple AM-GM gives that   $$ x_k\leq \frac{a_{k+1}}{\sqrt[k]{a_1a_2\dots a_k}} $$  So we have 
\begin{align*}
x_1x_{2}^2\dots x_{99}^{99}\leq \frac{a_2a_{3}^2\dots a_{100}^{99}}{a_{1}^{99} a_{2}^{98}\dots a_{99}}=\left(\frac{a_{100}}{a_1}\right)^{99}\cdot \left(\frac{a_{99}}{a_2}\right)^{97}\dots \frac{a_{51}}{a_{50}} \leq 1
\end{align*}
by the condition.  $\blacksquare$

I hope the following works. Every time we choose the constant here, it is always independent of  $p,q$ . The main motivation comes from the proof of the following lemma:**Lemma 1:** For fixed  $n$  non-negative integer, there exist a rational polynomial  $R_n(x)$  such that for any  $k$ ,  $$ [1^n+2^n+\ldots+(k-1)^n]=(k-1)R_n(k-1). $$   

<details><summary>Proof of Lemma 1</summary>Proof: This is done by strong induction. The conclusion is clearly true for  $n=0$ .

Suppose  $n\leq m-1$  is true and we want to show for  $n=m$ . Consider the following:
\begin{align*}
(k-1)^{m+1}=&\text{ }1^{m+1}+2^{m+1}+\ldots+(k-1)^{m+1}
&\text{ }-(1-1)^{m+1}-(2-1)^{m+1}-\ldots-((k-1)-1)^{m+1}
=&\text{ }(m+1)(1^m+2^m+\ldots+(k-1)^m)+(k-1)Q(k-1), Q\in \mathbb{Q}[x].
\end{align*}
which shows that  $n=m$  must be true as well.</details>

The first step is to characterize all the bad numbers. **<span style="color:#f00">Claim 1:</span>** Bad numbers are of the form  $px+qy$ .

<details><summary>Proof of Claim 1</summary>WLOG  $q>p$ . For readability, we introduce the function  $B(r,s)=rp-sq$ . Then it is not hard to show that all the bad numbers are as follow:
\begin{align*}
B\Big(\lceil\frac{q}{p}\rceil, 1\Big), & & B\Big(\lceil\frac{q}{p}\rceil+1,1\Big), & & \ldots, & & B\Big(q-1,1\Big)
B\Big(\lceil\frac{2q}{p}\rceil, 2\Big), & & B\Big(\lceil\frac{2q}{p}\rceil+1, 2\Big), & & \ldots, & & B\Big(q-1,2\Big)
& & & & \ldots
B\Big(\lceil\frac{(p-1)q}{p}\rceil, (p-1)\Big), & & B\Big(\lceil\frac{(p-1)q}{p}\rceil+1, (p-1)\Big), & & \ldots,& &  B\Big(q-1,(p-1)\Big)
\end{align*}
where each  $(x,y)$  corresponds to the number  $px+qy$ . 

So for  $p=5, q=7$ , the bad numbers written in these form look like:
\begin{align*}
3, 8, 13, 18, 23 
1, 6, 11, 16
4, 9
2
\end{align*}</details>

Let  $P(n)$  be the proposition that if  $S_n$  is the sum of bad integers raised to the power  $n$  , there exists a positive integer  $m$ , such that for any  $p,q$  as described,  $(p-1)(q-1)$  divides  $mS_n$ . **<span style="color:#f00">Claim 2:</span>**  $P(n)$  is true for all even  $n$ .

<details><summary>Proof of Claim 2</summary>Note that the sum of the following numbers raised to the  $n$ th power is equals to  $2S_n$ :
\begin{align*}
B\Big(1, 1\Big), & & B\Big(2, 1\Big), & & \ldots, & & B\Big(q-1,1\Big)
B\Big(1,2\Big), & & B \Big(2, 2\Big), & & \ldots, & & B\Big(q-1, 2\Big)
& & & & \ldots 
B\Big(1, (p-1)\Big), & & B\Big(2, (p-1)\Big), & & \ldots,& &  B\Big(q-1, (p-1)\Big)
\end{align*}

So for  $p=5, q=7$ , the array will look like:
\begin{align*}
-2,& & 3, & & 8, & & 13, & & 18, & & 23 
-9,& & -4, & & 1, & & 6, & & 11, & & 16
-16,& & -11, & & -6, & & -1, & & 4, & & 9
-23,& & -18, & & -13, & & -8, & & -3, & & 2
\end{align*}

So we just need to show that  $(p-1)(q-1)$  divides the sum of these numbers raised to the  $n$  times a constant. This sum is given by
\begin{align*}
&\text{ }B(1,1)^n+B(2,1)^n+\ldots+B(q-1,1)^n 
+&\text{ }B(1,2)^n+B(2,2)^n+\ldots+B(q-1,2)^n 
+&\text{ }\ldots
+&\text{ }B(1,p-1)^n+B(2,p-1)^n+\ldots+B(q-1,p-1)^n
=&\text{ }\sum_{i=1}^{n} c_i (1^i+2^i+\ldots+(p-1)^i)(1^{n-i}+2^{n-i}+\ldots+(q-1)^{n-i})p^iq^{n-i}
\end{align*}
Since for each component  $x$  of this sum there exist a constant  $c$  such that  $cx$  is divisible by  $(p-1)(q-1)$  using Lemma 1, the sum must be too, implying  $P(n)$  is true for  $n$  even.</details>**<span style="color:#f00">Claim 3:</span>**  $P(n)$  is true for all odd  $n$ .

<details><summary>Proof of Claim 3</summary>This is done by induction. We observe that if  $A$  is the set of all bad numbers , let  $L$ ,  $R$  be the set of the leftmost, rightmost bad numbers in the array written at the start. Then if we subtract  $p$  to all of elements in  $A$ , we obtained  $A$  including elements from  $L$  minus  $p$ , and excluding elements in  $R$ . Furthermore,  $L$  is  $\lbrace1, 2, \ldots, p-1\rbrace$ .  $R$  is  $\lbrace q-p, 2q-p,\ldots, (p-1)q-p\rbrace$ . This is motivated from the proof of Lemma 1 and will allow us to induct downwards from even  $n$ . 

We can prove  $P(1)$  quite easily with this: 
<details><summary>Click to expand</summary>\begin{align*}
\sum_{a\in A} a^2 &= \sum_{a\in A} (a-p)^2 - \sum_{l \in L} (l-p)^2 + \sum_{r \in R} r^2
                                 &= \sum_{a\in A} a^2 - 2p\cdot\sum_{a\in A} a+(p-1)(q-1)\cdot p^2- \sum_{l \in L} l^2 + \sum_{r \in R} r^2
\end{align*}
So
\begin{align*}
2p\cdot\sum_{a\in A} a &= (p-1)(q-1)\cdot p^2- \sum_{l \in L} l^2 + \sum_{r \in R} r^2
                                           &= (p-1)(q-1)\cdot p^2 - \frac{1}{6}(p-1)p(2p-1) 
                                           &\phantom{=}+ (p-1)\cdot p^2 - pq\cdot (p-1)p + q^2\cdot \frac{1}{6}(p-1)p(2p-1)
                                           &= (p-1)(q-1)p\Big(p + \frac{1}{6}(2p-1)(q+1) - p\Big)
\end{align*}
and thus  $P(1)$  is true.</details>

Now comes the painful part. We want to show for  $P(2k-1)$ , and will employ almost the same strategy for  $P(1)$  on  $P(2k-1)$ , by binomial expansion of  $(a-p)^{2k}$ . 

Suppose it is true for any lower  $P(n)$ . The sum we have in  $P(1)$  now looks like this
\begin{align*}
\sum_{a\in A} a^{2k} &= \sum_{a\in A} (a-p)^{2k} - \sum_{l \in L} (l-p)^{2k} + \sum_{r \in R} r^{2k}
                                 &= \sum_{a\in A} a^{2k} - 2p\cdot\sum_{a\in A} a^{2k-1}+(p-1)(q-1)\cdot c'- \sum_{l \in L} l^{2k} + \sum_{r \in R} r^{2k}
\end{align*} 
where  $c'$  is some rational number whose denominator is finite from induction. Thus we actually only need to show that there exist a positive rational constant  $c$  such that 
\[ p(p-1)(q-1)\Big|c\cdot\Big( \sum_{r \in R} r^{2k}- \sum_{l \in L} l^{2k}\Big)=c\cdot T \]
This is easy to show for  $p,p-1,q-1$  separately, but we are in trouble if  $p-1,q-1$  shares a lot of factors, or if  $p|q-1$ . We will proceed with a different strategy, by first finding  $\sum_{r \in R} r^{2k}- \sum_{l \in L} l^{2k} \pmod{(q-1)^2}$ . (Note: There ought to be a better way to do it than this)

<details><summary>Evaluation mod (q-1)^2</summary>Note that the sum can be rewritten as
\begin{align*}
&\text{ }\Big((q-1)-(p-1)\Big)^{2k}+\Big(2(q-1)-(p-2)\Big)^{2k}+\ldots+\Big((p-1)(q-1)-1\Big)^{2k}
&\text{ }-1^{2k}-2^{2k}-\ldots-(p-1)^{2k}
\equiv &\text{ }-2k(q-1)\Big((p-1)^{2k-1}\cdot 1+(p-2)^{2k-1}\cdot 2+\ldots +1^{2k-1}\cdot (p-1)\Big) \pmod{(q-1)^2}
\end{align*}
It is not difficult to show that a constant times  $(p-1)^{2k-1}\cdot 1+(p-2)^{2k-1}\cdot 2+\ldots +1^{2k-1}\cdot (p-1)$  is divisible by  $(p-1)p$  with help from Lemma 1. Hence, there exist a rational constant  $c_2$ , such that  $\gcd{\Big(q-1, p(p-1)\Big)}\Big|c_2\cdot \frac{T}{q-1}$ .</details>

