<details><summary>Motivation + Commentary</summary>I would be utterly appalled if anyone can solve this with just pen and paper. I had **massive** help from ggb, and even then took me a very long time to solve it. Anyways, from the start: my initial observations from ggb were that  $O$  is the circumcenter of  $EFMN$ , and  $C$  the circumcenter of  $\triangle MPN$ . Thus  $AC$  is the angle bisector of  $\angle BAD$ , from which we can throw away  $Q,R$  as it suffices to prove  $OP\parallel AC$ . Now diverting attention to  $S$ , we quickly note  $OC=SP$  thus the circumradii of  $\triangle PEF$  and  $ABCD$  are congruent; and if we want to prove the above observations perhaps we can try showing  $PS\perp MN$ ? Since  $OS\perp EF$  we have  $CP\perp EF$ , perhaps this can help with angle chasing. At this point a few other observations include quadrilaterals  $CMPN$  and  $SEPF$  are similar; triangles  $\triangle SOF$  and  $\triangle CNO$  are congruent. Efforts to prove these led me in a long chain of circular reasoning though. I even tried a homothetic shift from  $(S)$  to  $(O)$ , so  $P\rightarrow C$  and marked the images of  $E,F$  accordingly, which gave me many parallelograms and congruent triangles but nothing substantial.

Here I realised I have not used  $EO=OF$  much. A usual way to deal with midpoints is a reflection of another through it, forming a paralleolgram. Thus I marked the antipode  $A'$  of  $A$ . Here I ggb observed that  $OSA'P$  forms an isosceles trapezium; so we are done, if  $S$  lies on  $A'C'$ , the antipode of  $C$ ! Again, I added on futile efforts of proving these as I failed to effectively utilise the information that  $AFA'E$  is a parallelogram. So, instead I tried marking  $A'$  the reflection of  $C$  across  $AP$ ! This instantly gives us the isosceles trapezium  $OSA'P$ , and it remains to show  $O$  lies on  $AA'$ . Once again I could not do this, and realised I am not using the information regarding  $P$  lying on  $EN,FM$  at all. 

Thus I returned my attention to  $(EFMN)$ ; I recall Brokard's, which should help since  $O$  is the circumcenter by ggb observation, so I marked  $EF\cap MN$  first, where the angle formed with these two lines should be equal to  $\angle OSP$  as they are  $90^{\circ}$  rotations. Then I drew  $EM\cap NF$ ...... and everything fell in place. I was so excited I was already about to type my solution... when I realised I wasn't done. I forgot about  $AC\parallel OP$ ! It wasn't even quick; simple angle chasing just doesn't cut it. Knowledge of the ubiquitous  $HM$  line (which is  $OP$  in  $\triangle HEF$ ) did not seem to help either. I was distraught. Note that again this is a very specific configuration; if one takes  $\triangle HEF$  as reference and consider the problem "Given  $\triangle ABC$ , altitudes  $BE,CF$ ,  $D$  midpoint of  $AH$ ,  $J,K$  are reflections of  $D$  across  $E,F$ ." Not all the time do we have  $KB,JC$  intersecting on  $(DJK)$ .

Since  $(PEF)$  and  $(HEF)$  are reflections across  $EF$ , I then drew the circumcenter of  $\triangle HEF$ ,  $T$ , which is also the reflection of  $S$  across  $EF$ . Then it suffices to show  $T$  lies on  $AC$ ! But showing circumcenter lying on some random line is usually not easy; again direct angle chasing is unlikely to work out(which I still tried for some time anyway). I somehow had to use the information  $E,F$  lies on  $AB,AD$ , and that  $A$  lies on  $(BCD)$ . I tried drawing random circles to use Reim's theorem; to no avail. Retracing previous steps, I noted that we have  $CMPN~SEPF$ ; so  $\angle BCD=\angle ESF=\angle ETF$   $\implies \triangle BCD\sim \triangle ETF$ ! We are done with spiral similarity, but noting  $AETF$  cyclic produces a simpler solution. 

In retrospect I should have figured marking the orthocenter of  $\triangle PEF$  after noting  $\angle EMP,\angle ENP$  should be  $90^{\circ}$  and  $CP\perp EF$ . But problem solving doesn't always go that smoothly, does it? With that I would like to claim that this is the most difficult P1 I have ever encountered, despite its short solution(kind of reminds me of combinatorics problems whose short solutions might belie its difficulty when scores are taken eg most recently [this](https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c6h1863148p12608769) or IMO P3's the past two years. Also note my solution might not be complete as I am assuming *segments*  $EN,FM$  meet at  $P$  and other acute triangle assumptions.</details>

