<details><summary>Solution</summary>Let the intersection of lines  $A_0H_0$  and  $A_2A_3$  be  $X.$  If we place  $\triangle{A_0A_2A_3}$  into the Cartesian Plane such that   $$ A_0=(0,0), A_2=(0,12), A_3=(9,0), $$   it follows that line  $A_0H$  has equation  $y=x$  and line  $A_2A_3$  has equation  $y=-\tfrac{4}{3}x+12,$  so we can compute their intersection to be   $$ X = \left(\frac{36}{7}, \frac{36}{7}\right). $$   Thus, it follows that   $$ A_0X = \frac{36\sqrt{2}}{7}. $$  Now, let us consider the cross-section formed by the intersection of  $\mathcal{T}$  and the plane containing points  $A_0, A_1,$  and  $G_0.$  It is not difficult to see that this cross-section is in fact  $\triangle{A_0A_1X}.$ Note that  $\triangle{A_1B_0G_0} \sim \triangle{A_1A_0X},$  so it follows that
\begin{align*}
\frac{A_1B_0}{A_1A_0} = \frac{B_0G_0}{A_0X} &\implies \frac{5-s}{5} = \frac{s\sqrt{2}}{\frac{36\sqrt{2}}{7}} 
&\implies \frac{5-s}{5} = \frac{7s}{36} 
&\implies 180-36s=35s 
&\implies 71s=180 
&\implies s = \boxed{\frac{180}{71}}.
\end{align*} $\square$</details>

