<details><summary>Solution</summary>Let  $X$  be the midpoint of  $\overline{BC}$ , and let  $A_1$  be the point such that  $\triangle BA_1C$  is right isosceles with right angle at  $A_1$ , pointing towards  $\triangle ABC$ . We prove two claims, the first of which is well-known.**Lemma.** Quadrilateral  $AB_1A_1C_1$  is a parallelogram.
*Proof.* Let  $U$  and  $V$  be the midpoints of  $\overline{AB}$  and  $\overline{AC}$  (not in diagram). Since  $\triangle B_1AC \stackrel{+}{\sim} \triangle C_1BA$ , by mean geometry the midpoint triangle  $\triangle MUV$  is similar to both, and directly similar to  $\triangle A_1BC$ . Thus  $M$  is the midpoint of  $\overline{AA_1}$  too, done.**Lemma.** (Main claim) The triangle  $XB_1C_1$  is right isosceles with right angle at  $X$ .
*Proof.* Let  $Y$  be the reflection of  $C$  over  $B_1$ ; define  $Z$  similarly. Note  $\triangle A_1BC \stackrel{+}{\sim} \triangle CAY$ , so the midpoint triangle  $\triangle MXB_1 \stackrel{+}{\sim} \triangle A_1BC$ . Likewise  $\triangle MC_1X \stackrel{+}{\sim} \triangle A_1BC$ , so  $\triangle XB_1C_1$  is right isosceles at  $X$ , as desired.
