With these two claims, the remainder of the solution is easy. Since  $B_1C_1 = 12$ ,  $MX = 6$ . Now
\[BC^2 = 2\big(MB^2 + MC^2\big) - 4MX^2 = 2(7^2 + 11^2) - 12^2 = 196\]
and so  $BC = 14$ . By Heron's formula, the area of  $\triangle MBC$  equals  $\sqrt{16 \cdot 9 \cdot 5 \cdot 2} = 12\sqrt{10}$ . Finally, since  $M$  is the midpoint of  $\overline{AA_1}$ ,
\[[ABC] = 2[MBC] - [A_1BC] = 2 \cdot 12\sqrt{10} - \big(\tfrac{14}{2}\big)^2 = 24\sqrt{10} - 49.\]</details>

