It is easy to prove that  $AB_1A_1C_1$  is a parallelogram and  $\triangle C_1BA_1$  and  $\triangle B_1A_1C$  are similar to  $\triangle ABC$  with ratio  $\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}$ .

We compute the area of pentagon  $AC_1BCB_1$  in two ways. First of all, note
\begin{align*}
[AC_1BCB_1] & = [ABC] + [AC_1B] + [AB_1C] = K + \tfrac{b^2 + c^2}{4}.
\end{align*}
On the other hand,
\begin{align*}
[AC_1BCB_1] & = [AB_1A_1C_1] + [C_1BA_1] + [B_1A_1C] + [A_1BC]
& = [AB_1A_1C_1] + \tfrac{1}{2}[ABC] + \tfrac{1}{2}[ABC] + \tfrac{a^2}{4}
& = [AB_1A_1C_1] + K + \tfrac{a^2}{4}.
\end{align*}
Thus  $[AB_1A_1C_1] = \tfrac{b^2 + c^2 - a^2}{4}$ . However  $AB_1A_1C_1$  is a parallelogram with sides  $\tfrac{b}{\sqrt{2}}$  and  $\tfrac{c}{\sqrt{2}}$ , along with angle  $\angle A + 90^{\circ}$ . Thus
\[\tfrac{b^2 + c^2 - a^2}{4} = [AB_1A_1C_1] = \tfrac{bc}{2} \cdot \sin(\angle A + 90^{\circ}) = \tfrac{bc}{2} \cos \angle A\]
