Find all triplets  $(x,y,z)\in\mathbb{R}^3$  such that
\begin{align*}
x^2-yz &= |y-z|+1,  
y^2-zx &= |z-x|+1,  
z^2-xy &= |x-y|+1.
\end{align*}

candidates:
We have
\begin{align*}
2^a \pmod{120} &\in \{ 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64 \}  	
3^b \pmod{120} &\in \{ 1, 3, 9, 27, 81 \}  	
5^c \pmod{120} &\in \{ 1, 5, 25 \} 
\end{align*}
Residue class of  $2^a + 3^b - 5^c \pmod{120}$  have at most  $3\times 5 \times 7 = 105$  elements, so non-residue class of  $2^a + 3^b - 5^c$  have at least 15 elements. This means there exist  $n$  so that  $2^a + 3^b - 5^c \equiv n \pmod{120}$  have no solution. So every integer in form of  $120k + n$  for all  $k$  integer can't be expressed as  $2^a + 3^b - 5^c$ .

solution_md:
Since  $(a_n)$  is a sequence of positive reals  $a=\lim_{n \to \infty} a_{n+1}-a_n$  is positive. For each  $1>\epsilon>0$  there is a natural number  $N$  such that for all  $n\geqslant N$ \begin{align*}
(1-\epsilon)an&<a_n<(1+\epsilon)an \text{ and} 
(1-\epsilon)a&<a_{n+1}-a_n<(1+\epsilon)a 
\Rightarrow 1+\frac{1-\epsilon}{(1+\epsilon)n}&<\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}<1+\frac{1+\epsilon}{(1-\epsilon)n}.
\end{align*}
By squeeze theorem we get  $$ \exp\left(\frac{1-\epsilon}{1+\epsilon}\right)<\lim_{n \to \infty} \left(\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\right)^n<\exp\left(\frac{1+\epsilon}{1-\epsilon}\right), $$  so  $\lim_{n \to \infty} \left(\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\right)^n=e$ .

Lets fix  $ b_1,b_2,b_3$  and minimize  $a_1b_1+a_2b_2+a_3b_3$ Define  $h(a_1,a_2,a_3)=a_1b_1+a_2b_2+a_3b_3$ It is known that $ min$   $h(a_1,a_2,a_3)$ = $min$  { $h(c_1,c_2,c_3)$  :  $c_i \in$  { $0,\frac{1}{2}$ }}

