\begin{align*}
\pi_{kb} = \sum_{i=1}^n b_i
\end{align*}
Then as we vary over all  $k$ , or all permutations, we must show,
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}[\pi_{kb}] \leq n \cdot \left(\frac{n-1}{2} + \left\lfloor \frac{a_1+ \dots + a_n}{n} \right\rfloor \right)
\end{align*}
Now we will compute  $\mathbb{E}[\pi_{kb}]$ .

Consider some random  $a_i$ . Now lets say  $a_i \equiv k \pmod{n}$ . Note that  $a_i$  is assigned every residue from  $(0, 1, 2, \dots, n-1)$  exactly  $(n-1)!$  times. Furthermore if  $a_i$  is assigned a residue  $r$  then  $b_i - a_i$  is equal to the positive value of  $r - k \pmod{n}$ , ie: for  $k - 1$  we would have  $b_i - a_i = n - 1$ . Thus we find,
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}(b_i - a_i) = \frac{1}{n} \cdot \frac{n(n-1)}{2} = \frac{n-1}{2}
\end{align*}
Thus our total sum over all pairs  $(a_i, b_i)$  is  $\frac{n(n-1)}{2}$ . Now computing we have, 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}(\pi_{kb}) &= \mathbb{E}\left(\sum_{i=1}^n b_i - a_i\right) + \mathbb{E}\left(\sum_{i=1}^n a_i\right)
&= \frac{n(n-1)}{2} + \sum_{i=1}^n a_i
\end{align*}
Now noting that  $b_1 + b_2 + \dots + b_n - \frac{n(n-1)}{2}$  is always an integer divisible by  $n$ , we can optimize slightly more (the floor part) to have that there exists some choice of the  $a_i$  such that,
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{n} \cdot \sum_{i=1}^n b_i \leq \frac{n-1}{2} + \left\lfloor  \frac{\sum_{i=1}^n a_i}{n} \right\rfloor
\end{align*}

