From the above problem we know that if  $f(x)=a_nx^n+\cdots+a_1x+a_0$  with positive coefficient such that  $0<a_n<a_{n-1}<\cdots<a_1<a_0$  then all roots of  $f(x)$  lie out of the unit circle ,similarily all roots of  $g(x)=x^nf(\frac{1}{x})$  lie in the unit circle , now let's prove the following lemma: **All roots of  $h(x)=f(x)+g(x)$  lie on the unit circle**.**Proof**:Let  $f(x)=a\prod_{i=1}^{n}(x-z_i)=a\prod_{i=1}^{n}(x-\overline{z_i})$  where  $|z_i|>1,1\le i\le n$ , now consider any root  $z$  of  $h$ , we have
		\begin{align*}
			|\prod_{i=1}^{n}(z-\overline{z_i})|=|\prod_{i=1}^{n}(1-zz_i)|
		\end{align*}
notice that
	\begin{align*}
		|z-\overline{z_i}|^2-|1-zz_i|^2&=|z^2|+|z_i|^2-zz_i-\overline{zz_i}-1-|z|^2|z_i|^2+\overline{zz_i}+zz_i
		&=(|z|^2-1)(|z_i|^2-1)
	\end{align*}
if  $|z|>1$ ，then  $|z-\overline{z_i}|^2>|1-zz_i|^2$  which leads to the following contradiction
\begin{align*}
		|\prod_{i=1}^{n}(z-\overline{z_i})|>|\prod_{i=1}^{n}(1-zz_i)|
\end{align*}
Also for that case  $|z|<1$  we find that  $z$  is a root of  $h(x)$  if and only if  $\frac{1}{z}$  is also a root of  $h$ , done!
Now come back to this problem. Try to find a  $f(x)$  such that  $P(x)=f(x)+x^{2n}f(\frac{1}{x})$  Let  $f(x)=a_{2n}x^{2n}+\cdots+a_1x+a_0$ , then we have
for all  $0\le k\le n$ , $a_{n-k}+a_{n+k}=n+k$ ,now let
\begin{align*}
a_{n}=\frac{n}{2},a_{n+1}=\frac{n}{2}-\epsilon,\cdots,\ a_{2n}=\frac{n}{2}-n\epsilon
\end{align*} 
then 
\begin{align*}
a_{n}=\frac{n}{2},a_{n-1}=\frac{n}{2}+1+\epsilon,\cdots,\ a_{n-k}=\frac{n}{2}+k+k\epsilon,\cdots,a_{0}=\frac{n}{2}+n+n\epsilon
\end{align*} 
now choose  $0<\epsilon<\frac{1}{2}$ ,then  $f(x)$  satisfies the property we need, so all roots  $P(x)=f(x)+x^{2n}f(\frac{1}{x})$  lie on unit circle.

