I can't believe no one has posted this solution yet. I think it's hilarious.

Let  $*$  represent Dirichlet convolution, and let  $g$  be the Dirichlet series generating function of  $f$ . Let  $\zeta$  be the Riemann zeta function. We are given that  $f * f = 1$ , so  $(g(s))^2 = \zeta(s)$ . Then 
\begin{align*}
g(s) &= \sqrt{\zeta(s)}
&= \sqrt{\prod_{p \text{ prime}} 1 + \frac{1}{p^s} + \frac{1}{p^{2s}} + \ldots}
&= \prod_{p \text{ prime}} \sqrt{\frac{1}{1-\frac{1}{p^s}}}
&= \prod_{p \text{ prime}} \left(1 - \frac{1}{p^s}\right)^{-\frac{1}{2}}
&= \prod_{p \text{ prime}} \sum_{k \ge 0} \left(-\frac{1}{p^s}\right)^k \binom{-\frac{1}{2}}{k}
&= \sum_{n = p_1^{e_1}\ldots p_m^{e_m}} \frac{(-1)^{e_1+\ldots + e_m}}{n^s} \prod_{ i=1}^{m}\binom{-\frac{1}{2}}{e_i}
\end{align*}
meaning that  $f(p_1^{e_1}\ldots p_m^{e_m}) = (-1)^{e_1 + \ldots + e_m} \prod_{i=1}^{m} \binom{-\frac{1}{2}}{e_i}$ . (Note that the condition  $f(1) > 0$  means that we were supposed to take the "positive" square root, i.e. whichever one makes  $f(1)$  positive.)
Then  $2018^{2019} = 2^{2019}1009^{2019}$  so  $f(2018^{2019}) = \binom{-\frac{1}{2}}{2019}^2$ .
It is well known (ish? not too hard to prove) that  $\binom{-\frac{1}{2}}{n} = \frac{\binom{2n}{n}}{2^{2n}}$ , which gives the final answer  $f(2018^{2019}) = \boxed{\frac{\binom{4038}{2019}^2}{2^{8076}}}$ .

