Note that triangles  $QPC"'$  and  $ABC$  are homothetic. Thus,  $CC'', BP, AQ$  meet at a point  $L$ . So, it suffices to show  $LC, PB_1, QA_1$  concur. By Desargues on triangles  $LPQ, CB_1A_1$ , it suffices to show that  $LP\cap CB_1$ ,  $LQ\cap CA_1$ ,  $PQ\cap A_1B_1$  collinear. Let  $B_2 = BP\cap AC$ ,  $A_2 = AQ\cap BC$ , then we want  $PQ, A_2B_2, A_1B_1$  concur.

We claim that in general:**Lemma 2:** Let  $PQ$  be points in the plane with  $Q$  not on a side of triangle  $ABC$ . Let  $AP\cap BC = A_1, AQ\cap BC = A_2$ . Let  $BQ\cap CA = B_1, BP\cap CA = B_2$ . Then,  $PQ, A_1B_1, A_2B_2$  concur.

*Proof:* Let  $\ell = PQ$ . Fix  $\ell, Q$ , and vary  $P$ . We see that  $P\to B_2\to A_2B_2\cap \ell$  is projective, as is  $P\to A_1\to A_1B_1\cap \ell$ . Thus it suffices to show that these are the same maps, and we only need to check three values of  $P$ .

Case 1:  $P = Q$ . 

Then  $A_2B_2 = A_1B_1$ , so their intersections with  $\ell$  are certainly the same. 

Case 2:  $P = \ell\cap AB$ .

Then  $B_2 = A, A_1 = B$ , so  $A_1B_1\cap \ell = Q = A_2B_2\cap \ell$ .

Case 3:  $P = \ell \cap AC$  or  $\ell \cap BC$ .

These are the same, so just let  $P = \ell\cap AC$ . Then,  $A_1 = C$ ,  $B_2 = P = \ell \cap AC$ , so  $A_1B_1, A_2B_2$  both pass through  $P\in \ell$ .

This gives us four points, and at least  $3$  distinct ones: We see that  $Q$  is not on any of the sides so Case 1 gives us one point, then Cases 2, 3 give us at least two more points, since if  $\ell \cap AB = \ell\cap AC = \ell \cap BC$ , then  $AB, AC, BC$  concur, contradiction. This proves the claim.  $\Box$ Thus we are done.  $\blacksquare$ **Remark:** Case 1 is necessary in the proof of Lemma 2, as if  $\ell$  passes through a vertex then Cases 2, 3 only give us 2 points. **Remark:** Lemma 2 can also be solved via projective transformation, or by noting that it is simply **Pappus's Theorem** on the triples  $(A, B_1, B_2), (B, A_2, A_1)$ .

