\end{aligned} $$  By Ceva's theorem, lines  $CM$ ,  $A_{1} Q$  and  $B_{1} P$  are concurrent. **Part 2.**Point  $M$  is on the radical axis of  $\odot(CPP_1)$  and  $\odot(CQQ_1)$ . Actually, Points  $A$ ,  $B$ ,  $S$ ,  $C$  and  $T$  are concyclic since  $\angle BAC = \angle PP_1 C = \angle BTC$  and  $\angle ABC = \angle QQ_1 C = \angle ASC$ . With  $PQ \parallel AB$ , we can get   $$ \angle MPQ = \angle ABT = \angle AST  $$  That is to say,  $P$ ,  $Q$ ,  $S$  and  $T$  are concyclic. In other words, point M is on the radical axis of  $\odot(CPP_1)$  and  $\odot(CQQ_1)$ . 

From Part 1 and Part 2, we can know that point  $X$  is also on the radical axis of  $\odot(CPP_1)$  and  $\odot(CQQ_1)$ . Done.  $\blacksquare$

Let  $R$  denote intersection of  $AA_1$  and  $BB_1$ <u>Lemma</u> Let  $AC\cap QA_1=E$  and  $BC\cap PB_1=F$ . Then  $EF\parallel AB$ <details><summary>proof</summary>Let  $\frac{PR}{AR}=\frac{QR}{BR}=k$  so  $$ \frac{A_1P}{PA}=\frac{A_1R+RP}{RA-RP}=\frac{A_1R+k\cdot RA}{(1-k)\cdot RA} $$  By Menelaus' theorem, $\frac{CF}{FA_1}\cdot\frac{A_1P}{PA}\cdot\frac{AB_1}{B_1C}=-1$ and  $\frac{AR}{RA_1}\cdot\frac{A_1B}{BC}\cdot\frac{CB_1}{B_1A}=-1$ Therefore 
\begin{align*}
\frac{FC+CA_1}{FC} &=\frac{A_1R+k\cdot RA}{(1-k)\cdot RA}\cdot\frac{AB_1}{B_1C}
&=-\left(\frac{A_1R}{(k-1)RA}+\frac{k}{k-1}\right)\cdot\frac{AB_1}{B_1C}
&=-\left(\frac{A_1B\cdot CB_1}{(1-k)BC\cdot B_1A}-\frac{k}{1-k}\right)\cdot\frac{AB_1}{B_1C}
&=\frac{1}{1-k}\left(-\frac{A_1B}{BC}+k\frac{AB_1}{B_1C}\right)
\end{align*}

Since 
\begin{align*}
\frac{FC}{CB}&=\frac{FC}{CA_1}\cdot\frac{CA_1}{CB}
&=\frac{1}{\frac{1}{1-k}\left(-\frac{A_1B}{BC}+k\frac{AB_1}{B_1C}\right)-1}\cdot\frac{CA_1}{CB}
&=\frac{1-k}{-\frac{A_1B}{A_1C}+k\frac{AB_1}{B_1C}\frac{BC}{A_1C}-\frac{BC}{A_1C}+k\frac{BC}{A_1C}}
\end{align*}

Because
\begin{align*}
-\frac{A_1B}{A_1C}+k\frac{AB_1}{B_1C}\frac{BC}{A_1C}-\frac{BC}{A_1C}+k\frac{BC}{A_1C}
=k\left(\frac{AB_1}{B_1C}+1\right)\cdot\frac{BC}{A_1C}-1
=\frac{AC}{B_1C}\frac{BC}{A_1C}-1
\end{align*}
By symmetry, so  $\frac{FC}{CB}=\frac{EC}{CA}$ . $\therefore EF\parallel AB$   $\square$</details>

Let  $S,T$  be second intersection of  $\odot EFC$  with  $PB_1$  and  $QA_1$  respectively.
By angle chasing,  $\angle PSC=\angle FEC=\angle BAC$ .  $\therefore S=P_1$ . Similarly, $T=Q_1$ . And  $\angle STE=180^{\circ}-\angle EFS=\angle SPQ$ . 
Hence,  $P_1,Q_1,P,Q$  are concyclic.  $\square$

