Notice, by Legendre's:
\begin{align*}
k - s_2(n) = \nu_2(k!) = \dfrac{(n-1)n}{2}.
\end{align*}
Thus,  $k \geq \tfrac{n(n-1)}{2}$ . Then, by LTE, for even  $n$ :
\begin{align*}
\nu_3(2^n - 1) = \nu_3\left(2^n-(-1)^n\right) = 1 + \nu_3(n).
\end{align*}
For odd  $n$ :
\begin{align*}
2^n - 1 &\equiv (-1)^n - 1 \pmod 3 
&\equiv -1 - 1 \pmod 3 
&\equiv 1 \pmod 3.
\end{align*}
Thus, for odd  $n$ , then  $\nu_3(2^n-1) = 0$ . Hence:
\begin{align*}
\tfrac{1}{2} \cdot \left(k - s_3(k)\right) = \nu_3(k!) &= \sum_{j=1}^{\lfloor n/2 \rfloor}  (1+\nu_3(2j)) 
&=\left\lfloor \dfrac{n}{2} \right\rfloor + \sum_{j=1}^{\lfloor n/2 \rfloor}  \nu_3(2j) 
&=\left\lfloor \dfrac{n}{2} \right\rfloor + \left(\left\lfloor \dfrac{\lfloor n/2 \rfloor}{3} \right\rfloor + \left\lfloor \dfrac{\lfloor n/2 \rfloor}{9}\right\rfloor + \cdots \right) 
&\leq \dfrac{n}{2}+\dfrac{n}{6}+\dfrac{n}{18}+\cdots 
&= \dfrac{3n}{4}.
\end{align*}
Thus,  $k\leq\tfrac{3n}{2} + s_3(k)$ . Observe:
\begin{align*}
(3^n)! > k! \implies 3^n > k \implies n > s_3(k).
\end{align*}
Hence,  $k \leq \tfrac{3n}{2} +n =\tfrac{5n}{2}.$     
Combining, then  $\tfrac{n(n-1)}{2} \leq \tfrac{10n}{2}$  implies  $n \leq 11$ . We can test for all values and find the solutions are  $\boxed{(k,n) = (1,1), (3,2).}$

