Let  $L$  be the intersection of the external angle bisector of  $A$  and  $DI$ . The problem asks us to show that  $P,Q,L$  are collinear.

We begin by incircle inverting the entire problem. The inverted problem is stated below.
<blockquote>
Let  $DEF$  be a triangle with circumcenter  $I$  and circumcircle  $\omega$ . Let  $A$ ,  $B$ ,  $C$  denote the midpoints of the respective sides of the triangle. The line through  $D$  perpendicular to  $EF$  meets  $\omega$  again at  $R$ , and let  $P$  be the harmonic conjugate of  $R$  in  $EF$ . Let the circles  $(PCE)$  and  $(PBF)$  meet again at  $Q$ . Let  $L$  be the foot from  $A$  to  $DI$ . Show that  $P,I,L,Q$  are concylic.
</blockquote>
This labeling is quite unnatural, so the following is the same problem, but with a massive relableing.
<blockquote>
Let  $ABC$  be a triangle with circumcenter  $O$  and circumcircle  $\omega$ . Let  $X$ ,  $Y$ ,  $Z$  denote the midpoints of the respective sides of the triangle. The line through  $A$  perpendicular to  $BC$  meets  $\omega$  again at  $R$ , and let  $G$  be the harmonic conjugate of  $R$  in  $BC$ . Let  $L$  be the foot from  $X$  to  $AO$ . Show that the circles  $(GZB)$ ,  $(GYC)$ , and  $(GOL)$  are coaxial.
</blockquote>
[asy]
unitsize(1.8inches);
pair A=dir(115);
pair B=dir(210);
pair C=dir(-30);
pair D=foot(A,B,C);
pair E=foot(B,A,C);
pair F=foot(C,A,B);
pair H=extension(B,E,C,F);
pair R=2*foot(0,A,D)-A;
pair T=extension(E,F,B,C);
pair G=2*foot(0,A,T)-A;
pair X=(B+C)/2;
pair Y=(A+C)/2;
pair Z=(A+B)/2;
pair XX=(A+X)/2;
pair S=2*foot(0,A,X)-A;
pair Q=foot(H,A,X);
pair QQ=2*Q-A;
pair N=2*foot(circumcenter(G,Z,B),Z,Y)-Z;
pair L=foot(X,A,0);

draw(circumcircle(A,B,C));
draw(circumcircle(G,Z,B));
draw(circumcircle(G,0,L));

draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(A--R);
draw(B--E);
draw(C--F);
draw(A--T--B);
draw(A--S);
draw(A--(-A));
draw(N--Y);
draw(H--Q);
draw(X--L);

dot(" $A$ ",A,dir(A));
dot(" $B$ ",B,dir(250));
dot(" $C$ ",C,dir(C));
dot(" $D$ ",D,dir(135));
dot(" $E$ ",E,dir(45));
dot(" $F$ ",F,dir(F));
dot(" $R$ ",R,dir(R));
dot(" $G$ ",G,dir(120));
dot(" $T$ ",T,dir(-90));
dot(" $X$ ",X,dir(-120));
dot(" $Y$ ",Y,dir(Y));
dot(" $Z$ ",Z,dir(50));
dot(" $X'$ ",XX,dir(230));
dot(" $Q$ ",Q,dir(30));
dot(" $S$ ",S,dir(S));
dot(" $Q'$ ",QQ,dir(160));
dot(" $H$ ",H,2*dir(-60));
dot(" $O$ ",0,dir(45));
dot(" $A'$ ",-A,dir(-A));
dot(" $N$ ",N,dir(N));
dot(" $L$ ",L,dir(0));
[/asy]
We solve this restated problem. All angles are directed mod  $\pi$ .

Let  $DEF$  be the orthic triangle, let  $H$  be the orthocenter, and let  $T=EF\cap BC$ . Let  $X'=AX\cap YZ$  and let  $S=AX\cap\omega$ . Let  $A'$  denote the antipode of  $A$ . Finally, let  $Q$  be the foot from  $H$  to  $AX$  (the so called HM-point), and let  $Q'$  be the reflection of  $A$  over  $Q$ . We have the following key claim which drives the entire solution.**Claim:**  The pentagon  $GX'OLS$  is cyclic.

*Proof:*  First we'll show that  $X'OLS$  is cyclic. To see this, note that  $\angle XLA'=\angle XSA=\pi/2$ , so  $XLA'S$  cyclic, so \[AX\cdot AS=AL\cdot AA'.\] Dividing both sides by  $2$ , we see that \[AX'\cdot AS=AL\cdot AO,\] so  $X'OLS$  is cyclic.

We'll now show that  $G$  lies on this circle as well. To do this, we'll invert at  $A$  with power  $AH\cdot AD$ . Call this transformation  $\psi$ . Note that  $\psi(R)=EF\cap AH$ , so  $(EF;T\psi(R))=-1$ . Applying  $\psi$  to this, we get  $(CB;\psi(T)R)=-1$ , so  $\psi(G)=T$ . We also see that  $\angle HDX=\angle HQX=\pi/2$ , so  $HDXQ$  is cyclic so  $\psi(X)=Q$ , so  $\psi(X')=Q'$ . We also have that  $TH\perp AX$  by Brokard on  $BFEC$ , so  $T,H,Q$  collinear.

The key equality we're going to need from this inversion is the following. Note that  $\angle AGX'=-\angle AQ'T$  by the inversion. But we saw that  $TQ\perp AQ'$  and  $AQ=QQ'$ , so  $TAQ'$  is isosceles. Thus,  $\angle AQ'T=\angle TAX$ , so we have  $\boxed{\angle AGX'=\angle XAT}$ .

We wish to show that  $GX'OS$  is cyclic, or that  $\angle X'GO=\angle X'SO$ . Note that \begin{align*} \angle X'GO=\angle X'SO &\iff \angle AGO-\angle AGX'=\angle ASO  &\iff \angle OAG-\angle XAT=\angle OAS, \end{align*} which is certainly true. Thus,  $GX'OS$  is cyclic, so the claim is proven.  $\blacksquare$ The solution from here is easy. Let  $N=(GZB)\cap YZ$ . Note that \[\angle GNZ=\angle GBZ=\angle GBA.\] Furthermore, letting  $N=(GOL)\cap YZ$ , we have \[GN'Z=\angle GN'X'=\angle GSX'=\angle GSA.\] Thus,  $\angle GNZ=\angle GN'Z$ , so  $(GBZ)$  and  $(GOL)$  concur on  $YZ$ , so we're done by symmetry.

