Let  $T$  be the common midpoint of  $B'C'$  and  $DA'$ . Since  $\angle A'X'D=\angle A'PD=90^{\circ}$ ,  $DX'A'P$  is cyclic with circumcenter  $T$ . Observe also that  $IT\parallel HA'$  by the similarity between triangles  $A'B'C'$  and  $DEF$ . The exterior angle bisector of  $\angle PID'$  is parallel to  $PD'$ , so it must coincide with  $IT$ . Combined with  $TP=TX'$ , it follows that  $T$  is an arc midpoint and lies on  $(IX'P)$ . Note that  $Q'$  lies on  $B'C'$  because
\[\measuredangle PQ'C'=\measuredangle PEC' = \measuredangle PED = \measuredangle PFD = \measuredangle PFB' = \measuredangle PQ'B'.\]
Finally we have
\[\measuredangle PIT=\measuredangle IPD'=90^{\circ}+\measuredangle IPD=\measuredangle PED=\measuredangle PQ'T,\]
so we are done.

