We first prove that  $Q$  lies on  $(BIC)$ . Indeed

\begin{align*}
\angle BQC &= \angle BQP + \angle PQC = \angle BFP + \angle CEP = \angle PEF + \angle PFE 
&= \angle 180^{\circ} - \angle FPE = 180^{\circ} - \angle{FDE} = 180^{\circ} - \frac{\angle B + \angle C}{2} = 90^{\circ} + \frac{\angle A}{2} = \angle BIC.
\end{align*}

Now let  $QP$  cut  $(BIC)$  again at  $X$ . We claim that  $IBXC$  is harmonic. To prove this we prove that in fact it is similar to  $RFDE$ . We have  $$ \angle RFE = \angle RDE = 90^{\circ} - \angle DEF = \frac{\angle B}{2} = \angle IBC $$  Analogously  $\angle REF = \angle ICB$   so  $\triangle ICB \sim \triangle REF$ . Moreover  $$ \angle BIX = \angle BQX = \angle BQP = \angle BFP = \angle FRP $$  And similarly  $\angle CIX = \angle ERP$ , showing that the configurations are indeed similar. Henceforth we will ignore  $Q$  and prove that  $PX$  and  $DI$  intersect on the perpendicular to  $AI$  through  $A$ .

Now, in a great plot twist, we turn this incenter problem into an orthocenter problem by inverting about the incircle. Denoting by  $K'$  the inverse of  $K$ , we get that  $A', B', C'$  are the midpoints of  $DEF$ , the perpendicular to  $AI$  through  $A$  maps to the circle with diameter  $IA'$ , and  $X'$  maps to the midpoint of  $B'C'$  (Because  $BICX$  is harmonic!). Cleaning up unnecessary stuff and relabeling we obtain the following problem:

