Let  $A'$  be the  $A$ -antipode. First, it's not hard to give a better characterization for point  $P$ . Since  $PBRC$  is harmonic we have  $A(B, C; P, R) = -1$  and projecting onto  $BC$  we find that  $AP \cap BC$  lies on the line through the feet of the  $B$  and  $C$ -altitudes. Then PoP from  $AP \cap BC$  gives  $\angle PAH = 90^{\circ}$  so  $P, H, A'$  are collinear, and it's well known that this line also passes through  $M$ .

