Nice Problem! 

<blockquote>Let  $I$  be the incentre of acute triangle  $ABC$  with  $AB\neq AC$ . The incircle  $\omega$  of  $ABC$  is tangent to sides  $BC, CA$ , and  $AB$  at  $D, E,$  and  $F$ , respectively. The line through  $D$  perpendicular to  $EF$  meets  $\omega$  at  $R$ . Line  $AR$  meets  $\omega$  again at  $P$ . The circumcircles of triangle  $PCE$  and  $PBF$  meet again at  $Q$ .

Prove that lines  $DI$  and  $PQ$  meet on the line through  $A$  perpendicular to  $AI$ .

*Proposed by Anant Mudgal, India*</blockquote>**[color = red]Claim.[/color]**  $B,Q,I,C$  are concyclic. 

[color = blue]*(Proof):*[/color] We have \[\angle BQC = \angle BQP ~+~ \angle PQC = \angle BFP ~+~ \angle PEC = \angle FEP ~+~ \angle PFE= 180^{\circ} -~ \angle EPF = 180^{\circ} -~ \angle EDF = \angle BIC.\] Therefore  $B,Q,I,C$  are concyclic.  $~\square$ -------**[color = red]Claim.[/color]**  $P,A,D',I$  are concyclic where  $D'$  is the reflection of  $D$  in  $I$ . 

[color = blue]*(Proof):*[/color] It is easy to see that  $R$  is the reflection of  $D'$  in  $AI$ , so  $\angle PAI = \angle IAD'$ . Further we have  $IP = ID'$ . Now if  $AD' = AP$  then  $AR = AP$  or  $AR$  is tangent to  $\odot(DEF)$  which would force  $\triangle ABC$  to become degenerate. Therefore  $AD' \neq AP$ , which gives us  $P,A,D',I$  are concyclic.  $~\square$  ---------
Now we invert about  $\odot(DEF)$  using the above two claims which gives us this equivalent problem: 

<blockquote>
Let  $I$  be the circumcenter of  $\triangle DEF$ . The midpoint of sides  $EF, FD, DE$  be  $A,B,C$ . Let  $D'$  be the  $D-$  antipode in  $\odot(DEF)$  and  $DA$  meets  $\odot(ABC)$  again at  $P$ . The circle  $\odot(PCE)$  meets  $BC$  again at  $Q$ . If  $X$  be the feet of perpendicular from  $A$  on  $DI$ , show that  $Q,P,I,X$  are concyclic.
</blockquote>**[color = red]Claim 1.[/color]** If  $DA$  meet  $\odot(DEF)$  again at  $K$ , then  $P,X,I,K$  are concyclic and  $AD$  bisects  $\angle XKP$ . 

[color = blue]*(Proof):*[/color] Firstly we have  $\angle D'KA = 90^{\circ} = \angle AXD$ , so  $D',K,A,X$  are concyclic. Therefore we have \[\angle DIP = 2\angle DD'P = \angle DD'P ~+~ \angle XD'A = \angle DKP ~+~ \angle XKA = \angle XKP.\] This gives us that  $P,X,I,K$  are concyclic and also we showed that  $\angle DKP = \angle XKA = \angle DD'P$ , so  $AD$  bisects  $\angle XKP$ .  $~\square$ ---------**[color = red]Claim 2.[/color]** If  $J$  be the midpoint of  $AD$ , then  $J$  also lies on  $\odot(PXIK)$ . 

[color = blue]*(Proof):*[/color] As  $\angle DXA = 90^{\circ}$  and  $J$  is midpoint of  $AD$ , so \[\angle KJX = 2\angle KDX = 2\angle KDD' = \angle KID'.\] This gives us that  $J,I,X,K$  are concyclic so  $J$  also lies on  $\odot(PXIK)$ .  $~\square$ ---------
We need to show that  $Q$  also lies on the circle  $\odot(PXIKJ)$ . 

As  $P,J,X,K$  are concyclic and  $KJ$  bisects  $\angle XKP$ , from Incenter-Excenter Lemma,  $PJ = JX$ . Now, we have \[\angle PQJ = \angle PEC = \angle PD'X = \angle JKX = \angle JPX = \angle PXJ.\] This gives us that  $Q$  also lies on  $\odot(PXIKJ)$  which gives  $P,Q,I,X$  are concyclic and we are done.  $~\blacksquare$ [asy]
