We first show that  $Q\in\odot(BIC)$ .

\begin{align*} \angle BQC=\angle BFP+\angle PEC
=\pi+A-(\pi-\angle FDE) 
=\pi/2+A 
=\angle BIC\end{align*}
Hence  $B,Q,I,C$  are concyclic. Now we rephrase the Problem as follows.

<blockquote> Let  $ABC$  be a triangle with Incenter  $I$  and let the Incircle of  $\Delta ABC$  touch  $BC,CA,AB$  at  $D,E,F$  redpectively. Let the Perpendicular from  $D$  to  $EF$  intersect the Incircle at a point  $R$  and let  $AR\cap\odot(I)=P$ . Let the Perpendicular to  $AI$  at  $A$  intersect  $DI$  at  $T$  and  $TP\cap\odot(BIC)=\{Q\}$ . Then  $BPQF,CEQP$  are cyclic quadrilaterals. </blockquote>

Let  $\Delta UVW$  be the Orthic Triangle of  $\Delta DEF$  and  $H$  be the orthocenter of  $\Delta DEF$ . We claim that  $P=\odot(DH)\cap\odot(I)$  is the  $D-$  Queue Point of  $\Delta DEF$ .

Let  $\odot(DH)\cap\odot(I)=P'$ . So by taking Pairwise radical axis of  $\{\odot(DH),\odot(I),\odot(EF)\}$  and applying Radical Axis Theorem we get that  $\{DP',VW,EF\}$  concurs at a point  $\{X_A\}$ . So  $-1=(X_A,U;F,E)\overset{D}{=}(P',R;F,E)$  but  $-1=(P,R;F,E)\implies P'\equiv P$ . So  $P$  is the  $D-$  Queue Point of  $\Delta DEF$ .

Now we Invert the Rephrased Problem around the Incircle. So the Inverted Problem is read as follows:-

<blockquote>  $ABC$  be a triangle with medial triangle  $DEF$  where  $\{D,E,F\}$  are the midpoints of  $\{BC,CA,AB\}$  respectively. Let  $A'$  be the  $A-$  antipode of  $\Delta ABC$  and  $T$  be the foot of altitude from  $D$  to  $AA'$ . Let  $\odot(TOQ_A)\cap EF=K$ . Then  $CEKQ_A$  is a cyclic quadrilateral.</blockquote>

