Let  $Z\in\odot(ABC)$  such that  $AZ\|EF\|BC$ . We show that  $\{\overline{Q_A-K-Z}\}$ . Let  $K'=Q_AZ\cap EF$ . We show that  $K'\in\odot(TOQ_A)$  which will further imply  $K'\equiv K$ . Let  $AD\cap\odot(ABC)=R$ . It's well known that  $\{\overline{A'-D-Q_A}\}$ . By taking Pairwise Radical Axis of  $\{\odot(AD),\odot(DA'),\odot(ABC)\}$  and applying Radical Axis Theorem we get that  $\{AQ_A,RD,A'R\}$  are concurrent at  $\{X_A\}$  and clearly  $\Delta TRQ_A$  is the Orthic Triangle of  $\Delta AX_AA'$ . So  $\{D\}$  is the Incenter of  $\Delta TQ_AR$ . Notice that  $\odot(TQ_AR)$  is the Nine Point Circle of  $\Delta AX_AA'$ . So,  $O\in\odot(AXA')$  and as  $VQ_A=VT$ . Hence,  $V\in\odot(TQ_AR)$ . Let  $V=AR\cap EF$ . So,  $\angle ZK'V=\angle ARQ_A=\angle VRQ_A\implies K'\in\odot(VQ_AR)$ . So,  $\{T,O,V,K',Q_A,R\}$  are concyclic  $\implies K'\in\odot(TOQ_A)\implies K'\equiv K$ . So,  $\angle CQ_AZ=\angle ZAC=\angle ZKE\implies\{C,E,K,Q_A\}$  are concyclic. So Inverting back we get that  $CEKQ_A$  is a cyclic quadrilateral. So Inverting back we solve the problem.  $\blacksquare$ [rule]**<span style="color:#f00">Remark:-</span>** We had solved this problem long time ago but I was too lazy to type the Solution out. :P. Basically it was an Orthocenter kind of problem. The fact that  $P$  is  the  $D-$  Queue Point (For Queue Points see these two blogposts [Part 1](https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c701535h1754217_the_expoints_and_the_queuepoints__part_one), [Part 2](https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c701535h1812900_the_expoints_and_the_queuepoints__part_two)) follows directly from [2011 Brazil MO P5](https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c6h439544_concurrence_of_angle_bisectors) .This problem was not that tough as we thought yet quite beautiful and rich. :)

