<span style="color:#00f">**Claim**</span>:   $MQ$  and  $PL$  meet on  $\omega$ . 
<span style="color:#f00">*Proof*</span>: Observe that  $L$  is the midpoint of minor arc  $EF$  of  $\omega$ . So,  $\triangle DPQ \sim \triangle DLM$  as  $DP$  and  $DM$  are isogonal in  $\angle FDE$ . Hence, there exists a spiral similarity centred at  $D$  taking  $PQ \to LM$ .
This implies  $PL$  and  $MQ$  meet at  $(DPQ) \cap (DLM) (\neq D)$ , which implies the claim.  $\square$ Let  $MQ \cap PL \equiv G \in \omega$ . Now, observe the homothety centred at  $H$  with factor  $2$  maps  $\omega$  to  $\Gamma$ , which means the reflections of  $H$  about  $D$  and  $G$ , say  $X$  and  $Y$  respectively, lie on  $\Gamma$ .
Now, let  $GH \cap \Gamma \equiv S'(\neq Y)$ .

<span style="color:#00f">**Claim**</span>:  $DS'=DA$ , that is,  $S' \equiv S$ .
<span style="color:#f00">*Proof*</span>: Notice we have \[GH \cdot HS'=\frac{1}{2}YH \cdot HS'=\frac{1}{2}AH \cdot HX=AH \cdot HD\]implying  $AGDS'$  is cyclic by PoP. Now, by properties of a complete quadrilateral, in this case,  $BFEC$ ,  $(A,H;Q,D)=-1$ . Since  $\angle QGD=\angle MGD=90^\circ$ , we know  $GQ$  bisects  $\angle AGH$  and  $GD$  externally bisects  $\angle AGH$ , which implies  $D$  is the midpoint of arc  $AGS'$  of  $(AGDS')$ . Hence, we have  $DS'=DA$ , which gives  $S' \equiv S$  as desired.

Hence, we have that the lines  $SH,MQ,PL$  concur on  $G \equiv \omega \cap (DPQ)(\neq D)$ .  $\blacksquare$

