Let  $\angle{BCA}=\theta.$  Then, since  $CA=CD,$  we have   $$ \angle{DAC}=90^\circ - \frac{\theta}{2}. $$   Also, since  $\overline{AB}$  is tangent to  $(ACD)$  at  $A,$  we have   $$ \angle{BAD}=\theta. $$   Hence, we have
\begin{align*}
    \angle{ABC} &= 180^\circ - \angle{BAC}-\angle{BCA} 
    &= 180^\circ - \angle{BAD}-\angle{DAC}-\angle{BCA} 
    &= 180^\circ - \theta - \left(90^\circ-\frac{\theta}{2}\right)-\theta 
    &= 90^\circ - \frac{3\theta}{2}.
\end{align*}
Now, let  $F \neq A$  denote the second intersection of line  $AE$  with  $(ABC).$  Observe that
\begin{align*}
    \angle{BXA} &= \angle{BCA} 
    &= \theta 
    &= \angle{BAX},
\end{align*}
so  $\triangle{ABX}$  is isosceles. It results that the altitude from  $B$  to  $\overline{AX}$  is also the median from  $B$  to  $\overline{AX},$  so the fact that  $\overline{BE} \perp \overline{AD}$  implies that  $AE=EX.$  Then, since  $AE=CE,$  we can deduce that   $$ EA=EC=EX. $$   Therefore,  $E$  is the circumcenter of  $\triangle{ACX}.$  Also, since  $EA=EC$  and   $$ \angle{CAE}=90^\circ - \frac{\theta}{2}, $$   we have   $$ \angle{AEC}=\theta, $$   so we arrive at
\begin{align*}
    \angle{AEC}=2\angle{ABC} &\implies \theta=2\left(90^\circ-\frac{3\theta}{2}\right) 
    &\implies \theta = 180^\circ - 3\theta 
    &\implies 4\theta=180^\circ 
    &\implies \theta = \boxed{45^\circ}.
\end{align*} $\square$

