It is easy to verify that
\begin{align*}
AC = \cos \alpha, \quad
AD =\cos^2\alpha,\quad
AK =\tfrac{\cos^2\alpha}{\cos\theta},\quad
KD =\tan\theta\cos^2\alpha.
\end{align*}
Note that
\[
AI=4R\sin\tfrac B2\sin\tfrac {90^{\circ}}2=2\cdot\tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}\cdot\sin\tfrac{90^{\circ}-\theta}2=\sqrt{2}\sin(45^{\circ}-\tfrac{\theta}2)=\cos\tfrac{\theta}2-\sin\tfrac{\theta}2.
\]
Therefore,
\[AZ=\sec\tfrac{\theta}{2}\cdot AI=1-\tan\tfrac{\theta}{2}=1-\tfrac{\sin\theta}{1+\cos\theta}=\tfrac{1+\cos\theta-\sin\theta}{1+\cos\theta}.\]
We are given that,
\begin{align*}
\tfrac12(AK+KD+AD)=AC\implies& \tfrac{\cos^2\alpha}{\cos\theta}+\tan\theta\cos^2\alpha+\cos^2\alpha=2\cos\alpha
\implies&\tfrac{\cos\alpha}{\cos\theta}+\tan\theta\cos\alpha+\cos\alpha=2
\implies&AC=\cos\alpha=\tfrac{2}{\tfrac{1}{\cos\theta}+\tfrac{\sin\theta}{\cos\theta}+1}=\tfrac{2\cos\theta}{1+\sin\theta+\cos\theta}.
\end{align*}
Observe that,
\[AX=AC=\cos\alpha=\tfrac{2\cos\theta}{1+\sin\theta+\cos\theta}=\tfrac{2\cos\theta(1+\cos\theta-\sin\theta)}{(1+\cos\theta+\sin\theta)(1+\cos\theta-\sin\theta)}=\tfrac{2\cos\theta(1+\cos\theta-\sin\theta)}{2\cos\theta(\cos\theta+1)}=AZ.\]
Therefore  $X=Z$  and hence  $X,I,Y$  are collinear. In fact  $I$  is the midpoint of  $\overline{XY},$  now applying Sawayama's theorem on  $\triangle AEB$  we are done.  $\blacksquare$

