<details><summary>Solution</summary>If the three numbers are in arithmetic progression, then this means at least one of these numbers is divisible by  $3$ .

Since the three numbers are prime numbers, this means the smallest one should be  $3$ . Also, since the three prime numbers must all be less than  $13$ , we only have a two possibilities for the three prime numbers:  $$ 3,5,7\text{ or }3,7,11 $$  [rule]**Case I.** The remainders are  $3$ ,  $5$  and  $7$  respectively.

Let one such number be  $x$ . We have:  $$ \begin{cases}x\equiv3\pmod{11}
x\equiv5\pmod{12}
x\equiv7\pmod{13}\end{cases} $$    $$ \begin{cases}x\equiv3-22\pmod{11}
x\equiv5-24\pmod{12}
x\equiv7-26\pmod{13}\end{cases} $$    $$ \begin{cases}x\equiv-19\pmod{11}
x\equiv-19\pmod{12}
x\equiv-19\pmod{13}\end{cases} $$  This shows that all such integers which satisfy this are  $-19\pmod{11\cdot12\cdot13}$ . So, the smallest such positive integer which satisfies this is  $11\cdot12\cdot13-19=1697$ .
[rule]**Case II.** The remainders are  $7$ ,  $5$  and  $3$  respectively.

Let one such number be  $x$ . We have:  $$ \begin{cases}x\equiv7\pmod{11}
x\equiv5\pmod{12}
x\equiv3\pmod{13}\end{cases} $$    $$ \begin{cases}x\equiv7+22\pmod{11}
x\equiv5+24\pmod{12}
x\equiv3+26\pmod{13}\end{cases} $$    $$ \begin{cases}x\equiv29\pmod{11}
x\equiv29\pmod{12}
x\equiv29\pmod{13}\end{cases} $$  This shows that all such integers which satisfy this are  $29\pmod{11\cdot12\cdot13}$ . The smallest such positive integer which satisfies this is  $29$ .
[rule]**Case III.** The remainders are  $3$ ,  $7$  and  $11$  respectively.

Let one such number be  $x$ . We have:  $$ \begin{cases}x\equiv3\pmod{11}
x\equiv7\pmod{12}
x\equiv11\pmod{13}\end{cases} $$    $$ \begin{cases}x\equiv3-44\pmod{11}
x\equiv7-48\pmod{12}
x\equiv11-52\pmod{13}\end{cases} $$    $$ \begin{cases}x\equiv-41\pmod{11}
x\equiv-41\pmod{12}
x\equiv-41\pmod{13}\end{cases} $$  This shows that all such integers which satisfy this are  $-41\pmod{11\cdot12\cdot13}$ . So, the smallest such positive integer which satisfies this is  $11\cdot12\cdot13-41=1675$ .
[rule]**Case IV.** The remainders are  $11$ ,  $7$  and  $3$  respectively.

This cannot happen.
[rule]
From the four cases above, the smallest such  $N$  is  $29$ . The answer is  $2+9=\boxed{11}$ .</details>

