Since it is impossible that  $a=-b$ , we find that
\begin{align*}
\frac{F_5 (a,b)}{F_3 (a,b)} &= \frac{(a+b)^5-a^5 - b^5}{(a+b)^3-a^3 - b^3}
&=\frac{5ab(a+b)(a^2 + ab + b^2)}{3ab(a+b)}
&= \frac{5(a^2 + ab + b^2)}{3}.
\end{align*}

So we want  $a^2 + ab + b^2 \equiv 0 \pmod{3}.$  Multiplying both sides by  $a-b$  yields  $a^3 \equiv b^3 \pmod {3}$ . We find the classes
\begin{align*}
1^3 \equiv 4^3 \equiv 7^3\equiv 10^3 \equiv 1 &\pmod{3}
2^3 \equiv 5^3 \equiv 8^3 \equiv -1 &\pmod{3}
3^3 \equiv 6^3 \equiv 9^3 \equiv 0 &\pmod{3}.
\end{align*}

We suspect that the answer is  $4^2 + 3^2 + 3^2= \boxed{34}$  and indeed we check that picking any two numbers in a class works, as any two numbers in a class is separated by a multiple of  $3$ :  $$ (a+3n)^2 + (a+3n)a + a^2 = 3(a^2 + 3an + n^2)\equiv 0\pmod{3}. $$  EDIT: I didn't see the   $a\leq b$  condition. We actually have  $\binom{4}{2}+\binom{3}{2}+\binom{3}{2}=12$  choices for  $a<b$  by choosing pairs that live in the same class and then add  $10$  of the  $a=b$  to get  $\boxed{22}$ .

